{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373123,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610071938346486c0d6935d41f6\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me start by investigating the bug. The report says: when the model answers with two tool calls, and the first call's source contains a string literal with a single opening brace like `lv_open = '{'.`, PIA runs only the first call. The arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}) and the second call is lost.\\n\\nThis smells like the JSON parser (ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL or ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP) doesn't handle string escaping correctly \u2014 when scanning the arguments string, it finds the opening brace `{` inside the string literal `'{'` and starts treating things as JSON incorrectly, or it uses naive brace matching / substring extraction that fails when the JSON string contains a brace inside a string value.\\n\\nActually the symptom: \\\"arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\\" \u2014 that suggests the parser extracts the value of the \\\"arguments\\\" field but the value scan doesn't stop correctly, swallowing past the closing quote into subsequent JSON text. If the value contains a `{` inside a string literal... Hmm, let me think.\\n\\nThe tool call JSON from the model probably looks like:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}}\\n```\\n\\nor arguments as a string: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":..., \\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`.\\n\\nIf the parser scans for a closing quote to find the end of a string value, and the string contains escaped quotes `\\\\\\\"`, it should skip them. If the parser instead scans for braces, a `{` inside a string value would break it.\\n\\nThe extra text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` suggests the value ends at some later point. Let me just read the code.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. read ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL.\\n2. Find the parsing bug.\\n3. Write a red unit test in testclasses of the relevant class (probably ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP or JSON_UTIL).\\n4. Fix, activate, run tests.\\n5. Ensure ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR stays as-is (red).\\n\\nLet me read the two classes first, in parallel.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610071938346486c0d6935d41f6\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by reading the relevant classes \u2014 the LLM client that parses tool calls and the JSON utility.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_202610071938346486c0d6935d41f6\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228014378132436610\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228014378132436610\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228014378132436609\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228014378132436609\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":899,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":896},\"output_tokens\":540,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":481},\"total_tokens\":1439}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373129,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007193843a730ac7566754a1c\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The parsing uses zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced with iv_open/iv_close and split_entries. The bug: when the arguments JSON contains a string literal with a single opening brace (unbalanced), e.g. `\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open = '{'.\\\\n\\\"` \u2014 actually in the JSON the arguments value is a string containing escaped JSON, e.g. arguments = \\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\". Hmm.\\n\\nWait \u2014 for the responses API, the entry contains `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\": ...}\\\"` where the inner braces are escaped as \\\\\\\" and \\\\{? No, braces aren't escaped in JSON; only quotes. So the arguments string value is a JSON string that contains braces which are NOT escaped (braces are legal unescaped in JSON strings). So the raw body contains:\\n\\n`{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}`\\n\\nNow extract_balanced with key 'arguments', open '{', close '}': it must skip over braces inside string literals. If extract_balanced counts braces naively, the '{' inside the string literal (in `'{'`) would... Let's read zcl_pia_00_json_util.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007193843a730ac7566754a1c\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228001080913687040\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228001080913687040\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":3787,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":896},\"output_tokens\":299,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":280},\"total_tokens\":4086}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373238,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007193850a4948ec1a1014bbc\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now let me identify the bug. The report: model answers with two tool calls; the first call's arguments source contains a string literal with a single opening brace, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.`. In JSON, that brace is escaped inside the arguments string: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. Wait \u2014 actually a single `{` inside a JSON string does NOT need escaping. So the arguments value in raw JSON is a string containing `{`.\\n\\nNow parse_calls_responses / parse_calls_chat do:\\n```\\nls-arguments = unescape( extract_balanced( iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\n```\\nextract_balanced finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` then finds first `{` after it \u2014 but the first `{` is INSIDE the string value (the literal `lv_open = '{'.` appears inside the arguments string). Wait, the arguments value is a JSON string, so it starts with `\\\"`. The content `{\\\"name\\\":...` \u2014 hmm, in the responses API, `arguments` is a string containing JSON. So the entry looks like:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_1\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nextract_balanced with key `arguments`, open `{`, close `}`:\\n- finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` at offset.\\n- lv_rest = from there: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",...`\\n- finds first `{` \u2014 that's the `{` of `{\\\"name\\\":...` (the escaped one: `\\\\\\\"{`? no wait). The arguments string content is `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}`. The first `{` character in lv_rest is the opening brace of the arguments JSON. Good.\\n- Then it counts depth: increments on `{`, decrements on `}`. But the `{` inside the string literal `lv_open = '{'.` is also a `{` \u2192 depth becomes 2 when it should not. So the closing `}` of the arguments JSON brings depth to 1, not 0, and the scan continues past the end of the arguments string, consuming `\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` and even the next entry `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}` until depth reaches 0 at the `}` of the second entry.\\n\\nResult: arguments of the first call includes trailing junk `...\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",...}` up to balance \u2014 matching the reported symptom \\\"arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})\\\" \u2014 hmm, actually reported extra text is `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Hmm wait. Let me reconsider: the reported extra text after the JSON is `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. So arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":...source with '{'...}` + `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`? That means depth never reached 0 within... hmm, let's trace: if the source has an unmatched `{` (one `{` with no matching `}`), then the arguments JSON `{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"...'{'...\\\"}` \u2014 the scan starts at the outer `{` depth=1. Inside, the `{` from the literal increments depth to 2. The final `}` of arguments decrements to 1. Continue scanning: `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 that final `}` decrements to 0. So the extracted balanced text = `{arguments...}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 wait but includes also... the `}` at end. So arguments = `{...}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 hmm but reported extra text is `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 yes matches (maybe without trailing `}`, whatever). Then, since the extraction consumed past the first entry's end into... Actually it stops at the `}` closing the first entry (status completed). So lv_pos points at end of first entry. Then split_entries: it splits by brace counting too, with the same flaw! split_entries counts `{`/`}` without regard to strings. The first entry starts at `{`, depth counting: the literal `{` increments depth, so the first entry's closing `}` brings depth to 1, and it continues into the second entry until its closing `}` \u2192 depth 0. So the first \\\"entry\\\" swallowed the second entry \u2192 second call lost. That's exactly the bug: \\\"PIA runs only the first call... and the second call is lost.\\\"\\n\\nSo the root cause: brace counting in `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` ignores braces inside JSON string literals.\\n\\nFix: make both methods string-aware \u2014 skip characters inside quoted strings (handling `\\\\\\\"` escapes).\\n\\nFor extract_balanced: after finding the key, find the first opening char \u2014 careful: the opening `{` could be inside a string too. E.g., arguments is a string whose content starts with `{`. Hmm: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 in raw JSON the content braces are not escaped (only quotes are). So the raw text is `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"...`... wait no! In JSON, a string value cannot contain unescaped `\\\"`. So the arguments string content `{\\\"name\\\":...}` contains `\\\"` chars which MUST be escaped as `\\\\\\\"`. So raw: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. The `{` and `}` inside need no escaping. So after `\\\"arguments\\\":` we have `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...`. The first `{` found is the content's opening brace \u2014 correct. But if the source contains a string literal with `{`, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.`, that's just `{` in the raw JSON at that point. Fine.\\n\\nBut a subtle case: what if arguments value... the scan should start at first `{` after the key \u2014 that is the content `{`. Then depth counting must skip braces inside string literals *within the arguments content itself*? The arguments content is JSON: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Z\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... '{' ...\\\"}`. Within this content JSON, the `'{''` is inside the content's string value (source). So when scanning the arguments content as JSON, braces inside the source string must be ignored. So yes, string-aware scanning is needed in extract_balanced too.\\n\\nSo the fix: implement a helper that scans with awareness of double-quoted strings (toggle in_string on unescaped `\\\"`), skipping `\\\\X` escapes inside strings.\\n\\nLet me write a shared private... but JSON_UTIL is a utility class with all public static methods; I can add a new public method or just fix inline in both methods. I'll add a private static method `scan_balanced` maybe. Actually simpler: modify split_entries and extract_balanced to track in-string state.\\n\\nAlso extract_str uses PCRE `([^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*` \u2014 that's already string-aware. Good.\\n\\nNow: write a red test. Tests live in testclasses include of a class. Which class has tests for parse_calls? Let me check the testclasses of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. read_object returns \\\"main include, definition+implementation\\\" \u2014 maybe testclasses are separate; I can only read main via read_object. Hmm, \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation)\\\". The testclasses include is written via write_source with include='testclasses'. To see existing tests I can't read them... risky: writing testclasses would overwrite existing tests (like ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR mentioned \u2014 it's in some testclasses). ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is presumably in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL's testclasses. The user says it's already red and to leave it.\\n\\nI need to add a test reproducing the bug. Where? Best place: testclasses of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parse_calls_responses/parse_calls_chat) or ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (split_entries/extract_balanced). But I can't read existing testclasses to preserve them... Let me try read_object on the class \u2014 maybe it returns both includes? It says main include. Hmm. Maybe I can guess. Risky to overwrite.\\n\\nMaybe there's a dedicated test class like ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL has local test classes ltcl_json_util with methods like ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR. If I overwrite testclasses of JSON_UTIL I'd destroy ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR. The user said \\\"One existing test, ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR, is already red on this system... leave it as it is.\\\" That implies it must still exist after my changes \u2192 I must preserve it. So I need its source. Perhaps read_object returns it? Let me try reading and see. If not, maybe there's a naming convention: test classes might be in separate classes? \\\"Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\\\" So ltcl_ classes in the include.\\n\\nI'll try read_object for ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL \u2014 already did; it returned only main. Hmm, so I can't see testclasses. Maybe I can create tests in a class whose testclasses I can safely define fresh \u2014 e.g., put the new test into testclasses of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. But that class might already have tests that I'd overwrite... Unknown.\\n\\nAlternative: create a brand-new class? The write_source tool takes a class name \u2014 maybe it can create a new class? Possibly not. Let me think: the safest approach is to write testclasses for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP including a reproduction test. If LLM_HTTP already has tests, overwriting loses them. Hmm.\\n\\nActually, maybe read_object does include the testclasses but these classes simply have none? For JSON_UTIL, ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR exists somewhere \u2014 likely in JSON_UTIL's testclasses. Since read_object didn't show it, read_object only returns the main include. So JSON_UTIL has tests I can't see.\\n\\nHmm. Let me try reading a likely test class name? Test classes are local (ltcl_*), not global objects, so read_object won't find them.\\n\\nOptions:\\n1. Write new testclasses include for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP with my reproduction test. Risk: clobbering existing tests there.\\n2. Try to see if write_source to testclasses merges? Unlikely \u2014 \\\"Provide the complete source\\\".\\n\\nThe user's instruction: \\\"write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red\\\". They also mention ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is red and to leave it \u2014 that's a hint that run_tests will show it red and I shouldn't panic or \\\"fix\\\" it. It's probably in JSON_UTIL testclasses.\\n\\nGiven the tool constraints, I think the intended workflow is: write the test into the testclasses include of the class being fixed (JSON_UTIL or LLM_HTTP), and the grader knows existing tests. Since I can't read them, maybe the system's write_source to testclasses replaces, and the intended solution is to put tests in LLM_HTTP testclasses... but existing ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR must be preserved \u2014 it's in JSON_UTIL, a different class, so if I write tests only in LLM_HTTP's testclasses include, JSON_UTIL's tests remain untouched. But if LLM_HTTP already has its own tests, I'd clobber them.\\n\\nHmm, is there a way to discover? run_tests on ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP would report pass/fail counts \u2014 if there are existing tests, counts \\u003e 0 tells me something exists. Let me run tests on both classes first to see the current state. That's a safe read-only-ish operation. Actually run_tests runs on published active code; fine.\\n\\nLet me run tests for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL to see what exists. That informs where ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR lives (it'll be red) and whether LLM_HTTP has tests.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. run_tests ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL and ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parallel).\\n2. Based on counts, decide where to add the test.\\n\\nIf LLM_HTTP has tests I can't read... I'd have to reconstruct them? That's bad. Alternative: put my new test into JSON_UTIL's testclasses \u2014 but then I clobber ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR which must stay. Unless... I recreate it? I don't know its exact source. But the user says \\\"leave it as it is\\\" \u2014 recreating it approximately might change it. Risky.\\n\\nBetter: maybe there's a class specifically for tests? Let me look at the registry/tools? Hmm.\\n\\nActually \u2014 maybe read_object CAN return testclasses if... no, description says main include.\\n\\nLet me just run the tests first and see. If LLM_HTTP shows 0 tests, I'll add my test there safely.\\n\\nLet me also double check the executor to understand how calls are used, though not strictly needed. The bug is in parsing. The report says \\\"PIA runs only the first call\\\" \u2014 because the second is lost in parsing. And \\\"Its arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON\\\" \u2014 because extract_balanced overran. Both fixed by string-aware scanning.\\n\\nWait, actually let me re-check: with two calls, entry 1 = {...arguments with unbalanced '{' inside..., \\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}, entry 2 = {...}. extract_balanced for entry1's arguments: starts at content `{`, depth goes 1 \u2192 the literal `{` makes 2 \u2192 arguments' closing `}` \u2192 1 \u2192 continues into `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 the final `}` \u2192 0. So arguments = `{...}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 matches report exactly (\\\"extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})\\\").\\n\\nAnd split_entries: entry 1 starts at `{`; literal `{` \u2192 depth 2 at... wait order: depth 1 at start; inside arguments content there's the outer `{` of arguments \u2192 depth 2; literal `{` \u2192 depth 3; arguments close `}` \u2192 2; ... entry1 close `}` \u2192 1... hmm let me recount. Entry1: `{ \\\"type\\\":..., \\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{ ... '{' ... }\\\", \\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\" }`. Braces in entry1 text: entry-open `{` (d1), arguments-open `{` (d2), literal `{` (d3), arguments-close `}` (d2), entry-close `}` (d1) \u2014 not zero! So scanning continues into entry2: `{` (d2) ... `}` (d1)? Wait entry2 also has arguments with braces... In the general case entry2 is balanced: entry2-open `{` d2, args-open `{` d3, args-close `}` d2, entry2-close `}` d1. Still not 0! Hmm. Then it continues to end of array, depth \\u003e 0 \u2192 lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1, so ONE entry = whole rest \u2192 only 1 call parsed, second lost. Yes: \\\"PIA runs only the first call... the second call is lost.\\\" And the name extracted for entry1 is fine, arguments overran. Matches perfectly.\\n\\nNote the interplay: even if extract_balanced were fixed, split_entries would still merge entries. Both need the string-aware fix.\\n\\nNow implement:\\n\\nIn split_entries: track in-string state:\\n```\\nWHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n  FIND FIRST '{' ... (start)\\n  depth = 1, in_str = abap_false\\n  lv_end = lv_start\\n  WHILE lv_end \\u003c len-1 AND depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_end++.\\n    ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n    IF in_str.\\n      IF ch = `\\\\`. lv_end = lv_end + 1. \\\" skip escaped char \u2014 careful with loop increment\\n      ELSEIF ch = `\\\"`. in_str = false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF ch = `\\\"`. in_str = true.\\n      ELSEIF ch = `{`. depth++.\\n      ELSEIF ch = `}`. depth--.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n```\\nCareful: when in string and ch = `\\\\`, skip next char: since loop does lv_end = lv_end + 1 at top, I can just do lv_end = lv_end + 1 extra inside. That's fine in ABAP WHILE.\\n\\nHmm, but wait: the FIND FIRST '{' for the start \u2014 the `{` could be inside a string? At top level of an array, entries start with `{` outside strings. Before an entry, we're outside strings (position after previous entry's `}`). Except after my fix the previous entry ends exactly at its `}`. OK fine. But to be safe, the start search is fine.\\n\\nEdge: escape at very end \u2014 clamped by WHILE condition.\\n\\nIn extract_balanced: after finding key and first open char \u2014 hmm, one subtlety: the first `{` after the key could be inside a string if the value is a string that contains `{` before... e.g. `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{'x'...\\\"` \u2014 the content itself starts with `{` typically. But consider `\\\"output\\\":[...]` for arrays: first `[` after key. Fine.\\n\\nBut consider key = 'arguments', the search `FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open IN lv_rest` \u2014 lv_rest starts at the key. Characters between: `\\\":\\\"` then content. The first `{` is the content's opening brace. Good. But what if a preceding field's value contains the text `\\\"arguments\\\"`? FIND lv_pat IN iv_json finds first occurrence of `\\\"arguments\\\"` anywhere \u2014 could be inside a string value (e.g., the source code contains the word arguments in a JSON string?). Existing behavior; keep. Actually for robustness I could keep as is \u2014 don't overengineer; but the scan itself must be string aware:\\n\\n```\\ndepth = 1, pos = lv_start, in_str = false\\nWHILE pos \\u003c strlen(lv_rest) - 1 AND depth \\u003e 0.\\n  pos++.\\n  ch = lv_rest+pos(1).\\n  IF in_str.\\n    IF ch = `\\\\`. pos = pos + 1.\\n    ELSEIF ch = `\\\"`. in_str = false.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ELSE.\\n    IF ch = `\\\"`. in_str = true.\\n    ELSEIF ch = iv_open. depth++.\\n    ELSEIF ch = iv_close. depth--.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDWHILE.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 but there's a catch for extract_balanced with arguments: the value is a JSON string `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. The content braces are inside a JSON string at the OUTER level! Hmm. Let's think: entry text = `...\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...'{...'...\\\\\\\"}\\\"...`. The scan starts at the content's `{` (which is inside the outer JSON string). As we scan, we encounter `\\\\\\\"` sequences \u2014 with my in_str logic: at start in_str = false; first char after `{` is `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true; then `\\\\` \u2192 skip next (`\\\"`); then `name` ... then `\\\\` `\\\"` \u2192 the `\\\\` sets skip, so the `\\\"` doesn't close the string... Hmm wait: inside the outer JSON string, every `\\\"` is escaped as `\\\\\\\"`. So with in_str logic: `\\\"` opens in_str; then `\\\\` causes skip of next char; sequences of letters; then `\\\\` skips `\\\"`; ... The in_str stays true until an unescaped `\\\"`. The arguments content's own quotes are all `\\\\\\\"` (escaped) \u2014 the only unescaped `\\\"` is the one closing the arguments value at the very end: `...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 hmm: content = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}` then closing `\\\"` of the outer string. So the `}` (arguments close) appears BEFORE the closing `\\\"`. With my logic: `{` at start (depth 1); `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true; then everything is in_str until unescaped `\\\"`; the content `{` (literal in source, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.`) \u2014 but wait, in the raw JSON, within the source string, a `'` is just `'`, and `{` is raw `{`. So while in_str = true, braces are ignored \u2014 good, the literal `{` is ignored. The arguments closing `}` \u2014 is it inside in_str? Sequence: ...source content...\\\\ (escape) \\\" (closes source string inside content) , \\\" (opens next...) hmm this is getting complicated because the content is itself JSON with strings.\\n\\nLet me carefully write the raw text. Content JSON (what the model sends as arguments string):\\n`{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS z... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}`\\nIn the outer JSON, this string is encoded by escaping `\\\"` \u2192 `\\\\\\\"`:\\n`\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`\\n\\nSo the raw char sequence after `\\\"arguments\\\":` is:\\n`\\\"` `{` `\\\\` `\\\"` `n` `a` `m` `e` `\\\\` `\\\"` `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` `Z` ... `'` `{` `'` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `}` `\\\"`\\n\\nScan with my logic starting at the content `{` (depth=1, in_str=false):\\n- `{` start, depth 1.\\n- `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true.\\n- `\\\\` \u2192 skip next char (the `\\\"`).\\n- `name` \u2014 letters, in_str still true.\\n- `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"` (this pair is `\\\\\\\"` closing the name string in content).\\n- `:` etc.\\n- `\\\\` `\\\"` pairs skipped.\\n- ... the literal `{` inside source: we're in_str=true (inside `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`) \u2192 ignored.\\n- `\\\\` `\\\"` \u2192 skipped (closing of source string in content).\\n- `}` \u2192 in_str is false now! depth \u2192 0. Scan stops at the arguments closing `}`.\\n\\nSo the extraction returns exactly `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 the full arguments content including the outer quotes? No: rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len) where lv_start is the content `{` \u2014 so it returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}` without the surrounding quotes. Then unescape gives `{\\\"name\\\":...}` \u2014 correct JSON.\\n\\nThe in_str toggling works because escaped quotes `\\\\\\\"` are skipped. And braces inside content strings are ignored because we're \\\"in_str\\\" during them. And nested content objects `{` inside content but outside content-strings \u2014 e.g. if arguments had a nested object `{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"b\\\":1}}` \u2192 raw: `{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\":1}}`: `{` d1, `\\\"` in_str, `\\\\\\\"` skipped... `:` then `{` \u2014 are we in_str? After `\\\\` `\\\"` (closing \\\"a\\\" string) skipped... let me trace `{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\":1}}`: chars: `{` d1; `\\\"` instr; `\\\\`skip`\\\"`; `a`; `\\\\`skip`\\\"`; `:`; `{` \u2192 in_str? We're still in_str=true! Because the `\\\"` after `\\\\` was skipped, so in_str never closed. Uh-oh. That breaks nested objects in arguments!\\n\\nHmm. The problem: my state machine treats the whole content as one string because inner quotes are escaped. The `\\\\\\\"` escape-skip logic means in_str never turns off inside the content. So a nested `{` in content (outside content-strings) would be wrongly ignored \u2192 depth miscount \u2192 extraction too short/long.\\n\\nWait, but does the current un-fixed code handle nested? Currently without string-awareness: `{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\":1}}` \u2192 `{` d1, `{` d2, `}` d1, `}` d0 \u2192 returns whole. Correct by luck. With my naive in_str fix: `{` d1; `\\\"` in_str=true; then `\\\\`+`\\\"` skip... `:` ; `{` ignored (in_str); `\\\\\\\"` skip... `1`; `}` ignored? Let's see: after `{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":{` comes `\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\":1}` \u2014 `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, `b`, `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, `:1`, `}` \u2014 in_str still true \u2192 ignored! then `}` final \u2192 still in_str \u2192 ignored. End of string \u2192 depth still 1 \u2192 lv_pos = strlen-1 \u2192 returns everything to end. That's wrong (overruns).\\n\\nHmm wait, actually \u2014 does it return everything? WHILE condition pos \\u003c strlen-1; loop ends at pos = strlen(lv_rest)-1, depth\\u003e0 still, rv_ = from lv_start to end of lv_rest. For arguments being the last field, lv_rest ends at `}\\\"` + rest of entry. Overrun. Bad.\\n\\nSo naive in_str breaks nested braces in arguments. Need better approach: treat `\\\\\\\"` as a quote toggle too? Think about what we're scanning: the raw JSON where the arguments content quotes appear as `\\\\\\\"`. The correct model: we want to count braces of the INNER JSON (the content), where inner quotes are the `\\\\\\\"` pairs. So the rule should be: a `\\\"` preceded by `\\\\` is an inner quote (toggles inner-string state); a `\\\"` NOT preceded by `\\\\` is an outer quote (we're inside the outer string, where braces don't count... but actually we START inside the outer string).\\n\\nAlternative cleaner approach: count braces only for characters not inside the inner JSON strings, where inner strings are delimited by `\\\\\\\"`...`\\\\\\\"`. I.e., toggle state on the two-char sequence `\\\\\\\"`.\\n\\nLet me define: scan from lv_start (the content `{`). State: in_inner_str. When we see `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` \u2192 toggle in_inner_str, advance 2. When we see `\\\"` (unescaped) \u2192 this is the outer string's closing quote \u2192 we are leaving the arguments value; but that shouldn't happen before the matching `}` of the content... Actually the content's closing `}` always comes before the outer `\\\"` (content is balanced JSON). So upon unescaped `\\\"`, we can just stop? Or treat as end.\\n\\nHmm, but hold on \u2014 is extract_balanced used in contexts where the value is NOT a string? Yes! For `tool_calls` array (open `[`, close `]`) \u2014 that's a real JSON array, not a string. And `output` array in responses. And `function` object in flatten_tools. For those, values inside are normal JSON with normal quotes. So the scanning rule needs to work for both:\\n- Case A (array/object value): quotes are normal `\\\"`...`\\\"`, braces inside strings ignored.\\n- Case B (string value containing JSON, like arguments): inner quotes are `\\\\\\\"`, and the outer delimiters are plain `\\\"`.\\n\\nUnified rule: maintain a string-state that toggles on `\\\"` when NOT preceded by `\\\\`, AND ALSO toggles on `\\\\\\\"`? No...\\n\\nLet me think again. Actually a unified approach that works for both: track escape prefix. When scanning char by char:\\n- If previous char is `\\\\` (i.e., current char is escaped): if current char is `\\\"`, it's an escaped quote \u2014 in case A it's inside a string (stay in string), in case B it's a delimiter of an inner string. Hmm, in case B, when we start at the content `{`, we are INSIDE the outer string. Inner string delimiters are `\\\\\\\"`. So state transitions in case B happen exactly on `\\\\\\\"` sequences. In case A, transitions happen on `\\\"` not preceded by `\\\\`.\\n\\nSo the difference: in case B, escaped quotes toggle; in case A, unescaped quotes toggle. Can't distinguish locally... unless: in case B, we start inside a string (the outer one). Hmm \u2014 what if the rule is: a `\\\\\\\"` toggles \\\"inner\\\" state, and an unescaped `\\\"` also toggles? In case B: start in outer string. First `\\\\\\\"` toggles inner=true (entering \\\"name\\\" string). The closing `\\\\\\\"` of name toggles inner=false. An unescaped `\\\"` would be the outer close \u2014 occurs only after content ends. In case B we never encounter unescaped `\\\"` before the end. In case A: unescaped `\\\"` toggles; `\\\\\\\"` inside strings \u2014 toggles wrongly!\\n\\nCase A example: `{\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"}}` \u2014 hmm wait, would `\\\\\\\"` appear inside? `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"}` \u2014 scanning the object: `{` d1; `\\\"` \u2192 toggle s=true; `name`; `\\\"` \u2192 s=false; `:`; `\\\"` s=true; `a`; `\\\\` + `\\\"` \u2014 if `\\\\\\\"` toggles \u2192 s=false \u2014 WRONG, still inside the value string.\\n\\nSo a single pass can't distinguish. Hmm.\\n\\nBUT: do we need case B at all for extract_balanced? Its uses:\\n1. parse_calls_chat: `tool_calls` array (case A), then per entry `arguments` (case B).\\n2. parse_calls_responses: `output` array (case A), per entry `arguments` (case B).\\n3. flatten_tools: `function` object (case A).\\n4. build_input_items: `tool_calls` (case A).\\n5. chat answer: `output` array (case A).\\n\\nSo case B is only for `arguments` with open `{` close `}`. Option: give extract_balanced a new optional parameter, e.g. `iv_value_is_string` (or handle generically: when the first non-space char after the key's colon is `\\\"`, then the value is a string \u2192 scan for inner JSON with `\\\\\\\"` toggles). That's clean and generic! Look at the char right after `key\\\":` \u2014 if it's `\\\"`, it's a string value; the inner content braces are escaped-quoted.\\n\\nHmm, wait. Actually simpler idea: for the arguments case, instead of brace counting on raw text, we could extract the string value with extract_str (`\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"...\\\"` PCRE handles escapes) and then unescape it. That's exactly what build_input_items does: `extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' )` which returns the ESCAPED string content, then... it re-embeds it. And in chat parsing, unescape(extract_balanced(...)) is used. Note extract_str's PCRE `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)` correctly matches a JSON string with escapes. So for arguments: `ls-arguments = unescape( extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) )`. That handles the `'{''` case perfectly! And the braces never need counting.\\n\\nWait but careful: extract_str returns the raw escaped content; unescape converts `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 `\\\"`, `\\\\n` \u2192 newline, etc. That yields the actual arguments JSON string. That's simpler and more robust than balanced scanning.\\n\\nHmm, but is there a reason they used extract_balanced for arguments? Maybe because arguments may be very long and PCRE... The PCRE FIND with submatches on long strings should be fine. Or maybe because unescape of `\\\\\\\"` etc. Both approaches unescape. Actually wait \u2014 current code does `unescape( extract_balanced(...) )` \u2014 extract_balanced returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}` raw with escapes, then unescape. extract_str returns the same raw escaped content `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}` (without needing brace counting), then unescape. Same result, but robust. \\n\\nOne difference: if arguments were sent as an actual JSON object (not a string) \u2014 some providers? OpenAI sends arguments as a string. extract_balanced would handle an object value; extract_str would fail. The mock/replay uses the same code. Hmm, risk: executor's mock LLM? Let me check if there's a mock that builds responses with arguments as object. Let me look at the executor and tools to see. Also ZCL_PIA_15_T_* tools and the mock. Let me check zcl_pia_00_executor quickly and any test double for llm.\\n\\nActually, to be safe, I can do: try extract_str first; if empty, fall back to extract_balanced (fixed version). But minimal, clean fix is better.\\n\\nBut we still MUST fix split_entries (entries merging) \u2014 that's where the second call is lost. And extract_balanced is also used for arrays (case A) where string-awareness is needed too: e.g. the `output` array containing a message item whose text contains `'{'`? For arrays: `\\\"output\\\":[{...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\"},{...}]` \u2014 split_entries on the array content must not count braces inside strings. So split_entries needs case-A string awareness (normal quotes with escapes). That's straightforward: toggle on unescaped `\\\"`, skip `\\\\X`.\\n\\nAnd extract_balanced for arrays/objects (case A) also needs it: e.g. extracting `output` array \u2014 brace/bracket counting could be fooled by `[` or `{` inside string values. E.g. an output_text containing `'{'`... for `[`/`]` counting, a `{` inside a string doesn't matter unless it's `[`/`]`. Text with `[` in it (like ABAP internal table decl?) could break it. So yes, fix extract_balanced for case A with standard string awareness (toggle on unescaped `\\\"`).\\n\\nFor case B (arguments), use extract_str. But extract_balanced signature stays; I'll fix its scanning to be string-aware (case A), and change the arguments extraction in parse_calls_chat/parse_calls_responses to use extract_str. Hmm \u2014 but wait: would extract_str-based arguments change behavior for the replay path or executor? The executor stores arguments and passes to tools. Let me check the executor to be sure about how it re-parses arguments.\\n\\nAlso `ev_assistant_raw` building uses escape(arguments) \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAnd build_input_items uses extract_str for arguments already \u2014 consistent.\\n\\nNow the fix locations:\\n1. `zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003esplit_entries` \u2014 string-aware brace counting.\\n2. `zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced` \u2014 string-aware counting (case A: normal quotes).\\n3. `zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat` and `parse_calls_responses` \u2014 arguments via extract_str + unescape (fallback to balanced if empty?). I'll do: extract_str; if initial, use extract_balanced (for object-valued arguments robustness).\\n\\nNow the test. Where to put it? Let me first run_tests on both classes to see existing test counts, and read the executor to be thorough. Let me do: read_object ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR + run_tests ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL + run_tests ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP in parallel. Hmm, run_tests might be heavy but fine.\\n\\nActually wait \u2014 there's a concern: run_tests runs on published active code; ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is red currently, expected. Fine.\\n\\nLet me also consider: maybe the test should reproduce at the parse_calls_responses level (two calls, first has `'{''` in source): assert lines( rt_ ) = 2 and rt_[1]-arguments equals expected JSON. That's the user-level reproduction. Plus maybe split_entries unit test. I'll write the test in LLM_HTTP's testclasses (if empty) \u2014 need to check first.\\n\\nLet me fire the parallel calls.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007193850a4948ec1a1014bbc\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by examining the parsing code paths involved and checking the current test state.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007193850a4948ec1a1014bbc\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228015718162233114\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228015718162233114\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228015718162233113\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228015718162233113\",\"name\":\"run_tests\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228015718162233112\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228015718162233112\",\"name\":\"run_tests\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6561,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":3776},\"output_tokens\":7647,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":7576},\"total_tokens\":14208}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373249,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007194040ecb3d724f38c4764\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now let me check the testclasses of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (or maybe ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL) to see where the tests should live. The tools available only read the \\\"main include, definition+implementation\\\". Hmm, read_object says \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation).\\\" So I can't read testclasses directly? The tool description says main include. Maybe testclasses include is not readable... Let me try reading the testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL via read_object \u2014 it says main. There's no parameter for include. So I can only read main. But write_source supports include testclasses. Since I must not clobber existing tests when writing the testclasses include (e.g., ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR in json_util), I need to know the existing test source.\\n\\nHmm, that's a problem: writing testclasses for a class would overwrite existing tests including ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR. The bug report says ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is already red and leave as-is. So if I write to json_util testclasses I'd delete it. I need to preserve it. But I can't read it...\\n\\nWait \u2014 maybe read_object returns the whole class including testclasses if present? It says \\\"main include, definition+implementation\\\". Let me just try reading a class and see. Let me read ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL again \u2014 I already did, no testclasses included. Hmm.\\n\\nLet me try reading some likely test class names. Maybe tests are in separate local test classes included in the testclasses include. I could create a new test class for parse_calls_chat tests in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP's testclasses include. But if that include already has tests, I'd overwrite them.\\n\\nAlternative: put the test in a new class? The instructions say \\\"Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\\\" I could write my test into the testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. Risk: overwriting existing tests there. But maybe there are none... Actually maybe there are (e.g., tests for flatten_tools or escape). Hmm.\\n\\nLet me try read_object on ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP to see if testclasses appear. Already did read it: no testclasses. So read_object only returns main include.\\n\\nHmm, is there a way to see the testclasses? The tools are limited. Perhaps run_tests on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL to see test names; the output might list failing test names and maybe count of tests. That would tell me which classes have tests. Let me run tests on json_util and llm_http to see what's there.\\n\\nLet's do that: run_tests(ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL) and run_tests(ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP) in parallel.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007194040ecb3d724f38c4764\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UT