{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373319,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_2026100719405386fce695a51c4ddc\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The LLM_HTTP has no test classes currently? \\\"classes\\\":0. Hmm, maybe test classes exist elsewhere. Let me check - run_tests says 0 classes. So ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP has no test include yet, or its testclasses include is empty. Let me check the testclasses include by... there's no read tool for testclasses directly. read_object reads main include only. Hmm. Actually the description says \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation)\\\". So I can't read testclasses directly. I could try write_source with include testclasses - but I need full source. Since run_tests shows 0 classes, the testclasses include is presumably empty. But careful: maybe there are tests but not run. Let me write my new test into the testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP.\\n\\nWait \u2014 but JSON_UTIL tests exist in its testclasses include; if I write to JSON_UTIL testclasses I'd wipe them. I don't need to touch JSON_UTIL tests. The fix is in extract_balanced. I'll add a test class in LLM_HTTP testclasses: LTCL_LLM_HTTP with tests for parse_calls_responses with the two-calls brace-literal scenario, and maybe a direct extract_balanced test... but direct test of extract_balanced would need to go in JSON_UTIL testclasses, which I can't read. So put everything in LLM_HTTP testclasses (parse_calls_responses / parse_calls_chat level), which reproduces the bug end-to-end at the parser level.\\n\\nAlso a test for extract_balanced string-awareness could be added indirectly through parse_calls: a source containing a single closing brace etc.\\n\\nNow the failing test (red first). Write testclasses include with the new test, run \u2192 red (only 1 call, arguments containing trailing junk). Then fix extract_balanced, activate, run \u2192 green.\\n\\nAlso note: there might be a risk that testclasses include already has content that read_object doesn't show and run_tests reports 0 classes because... hmm, \\\"classes\\\":0 strongly suggests empty. But let me be careful: maybe tests exist but are not local friends? No \u2014 local test classes in the testclasses include would be counted. I'll assume empty. Actually wait, to be safe, I could first write a testclasses include containing my test only; if there were previous tests they'd be lost. run_tests reported 0 methods for LLM_HTTP, so nothing to lose.\\n\\nLet me now write the test:\\n\\n```abap\\nCLASS ltcl_llm_http DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  DURATION SHORT\\n  RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_literal FOR TESTING RAISING cx_static_check.\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_literal FOR TESTING RAISING cx_static_check.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_llm_http IMPLEMENTATION.\\n\\n  METHOD responses_two_calls_brace_literal.\\n    \\\" arguments arrive as a JSON *string* whose escaped source contains an unbalanced\\n    \\\" opening brace ('{'): the balanced scan must ignore braces inside string literals\\n    DATA lv_src1 TYPE string.\\n    lv_src1 = 'DATA lv_open TYPE string.' \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\n| \\u0026\\u0026 'lv_open = ''{''.'.\\n    \\\" escaped the way the API sends it: \\\"...\\\" with \\\\n and \\\\\\\" inside\\n    DATA lv_args1 TYPE string.\\n    lv_args1 = '{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026\\n               'DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = \\\\x27{\\\\x27.' \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"}'.\\n```\\n\\nHmm, getting the escaped literal right in ABAP: I want the JSON body string to contain, inside arguments, the characters: backslash-n for newline, backslash-quote for quote. In ABAP string literal with backticks: `DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.` \u2014 with backticks, backslash is literal and single quotes are literal. \\n\\nSo the arguments value in the body is the text between the quotes:\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\"}\\n\\nThen unescape gives: {\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\u003cnewline\\u003elv_open = '{'.\\\"}\\n\\nGood. And arguments = '{' \\u0026\\u0026 ... Let me just build with backticks:\\n\\nlv_args1 = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\"}`.\\n\\nNow the body:\\n\\nlv_body = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"output\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          escaped args1 inner... \\n\\nhmm careful: arguments value is itself a JSON string containing the args JSON escaped. So body contains: \\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n\\nSo I need the args1 string escaped as a JSON string value. I can build it via a helper in the test: escape the raw args with zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eescape? escape() escapes backslash, quote, control chars, non-ascii \u2192 `\\\\\\\"` for quote. That's exactly JSON-string escaping. But escape() of `'{'` stays `'{'`. And newline \u2192 \\\\n. \\n\\nSo in the test:\\n\\nDATA lv_args1 TYPE string.\\nlv_args1 = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 `}`.\\n\\nWait but inside backticks, `\\\\n` is two chars backslash+n \u2014 but escape() would escape the backslash to `\\\\\\\\`. I want a literal backslash-n in the final body (representing newline). So don't use escape() on pre-escaped text; instead build raw args with real newline (|...| with \\\\n as newline? In |...| string templates, \\\\n is not an escape; newline must be inserted differently). Simplest: build raw args with real newline char:\\n\\nDATA lv_nl TYPE string.\\nlv_nl = cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline.  \\\" hmm, or |\\\\n|? \\n\\nIn ABAP string templates, there's no \\\\n escape... Actually there IS: in string templates, control characters can be inserted... no. |\\\\n| is literal backslash n? Hmm, no: In ABAP string templates, backslash isn't an escape char, so |\\\\n| is \\\"\\\\n\\\" two chars? Wait, actually in string templates `\\\\n` \u2014 hmm. Let me think: string templates use | ... | with escapes for { } via \\\\{ \\\\} and \\\\\\\\ for backslash. So |\\\\n| would be backslash followed by n. Hmm, actually in the code above (escape method), they check `IF lv_ch = |\\\\n|` \u2014 comparing a char to |\\\\n| and expecting newline! Interesting: in ABAP string templates, \\\\n IS a newline escape? Let me recall: Yes! In string templates, there are escapes: \\\\\\\\ (backslash), \\\\| , \\\\{, \\\\}, and also... hmm. Actually I recall that in string templates you cannot write \\\\n for newline; you use cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline. But the existing code `IF lv_ch = |\\\\n|` \u2014 if |\\\\n| were backslash+n, that comparison would never be true for a single char lv_ch... and ESCAPE_NEWLINE test passes. So |\\\\n| must be newline. Hmm wait, does open-ABAP/abapGit? Let me recall ABAP docs: String Templates \u2014 \\\"Escape characters: \\\\\\\\, \\\\`, \\\\|, \\\\{, \\\\}\\\". No \\\\n. But then how does ESCAPE_NEWLINE pass?\\n\\nHmm, maybe lv_ch is a 1-char string and |\\\\n| is... In ABAP, comparing strings of different length: 'x' = 'xy' is false. If |\\\\n| were 2 chars, comparison false, test ESCAPE_NEWLINE would fail. It passes. So |\\\\n| is newline \u2014 hmm, or maybe the test doesn't actually contain a newline. Let me not go down this hole; the code base presumably works.\\n\\nActually! I remember now: since ABAP 7.40? No... Hmm. There IS a thing: in string templates, you can embed line breaks by literally writing the template across lines. But \\\\n? Let me check: the escape method ALSO handles `\\\\r`, `\\\\t` via |\\\\r|, |\\\\t|. And UNESCAPE_CTRL passes. Hmm wait, maybe those comparisons are comparing against backslash sequences? In unescape, they use |\\\\n| in rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\n|. for `\\\\n` \u2192 newline. And test UNESCAPE_CTRL passes. So in this environment |\\\\n| = newline character. OK so open-abap (and apparently real ABAP?) \u2014 hmm, actually I'm now fairly certain real ABAP does NOT support \\\\n in string templates... but wait, maybe it does? Let me think about cl_abap_char_utilities... In many systems, people write |\\\\n| and it produces... Hmm. I recall ABAP string template escape sequences are only \\\\\\\\, \\\\`, \\\\|, \\\\{, \\\\}. So |\\\\n| would be backslash+n (2 chars). Then unescape `\\\\n` would append 2 chars \u2014 the test would fail... unless the test expects that.\\n\\nI shouldn't rabbit-hole; I'll avoid depending on it: I can use cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline in my test... but if the escape method relies on |\\\\n| comparisons being a single newline char, whatever. For the test body, what matters is the body text I construct explicitly. I'll build everything with backticks and concatenation of explicit `\\\\n` (backslash n, 2 chars) via `...` backtick literals \u2014 no newline chars at all. That's cleanest: the recorded body contains \\\\n as escape sequence, no real newlines.\\n\\nSo body:\\n\\n```\\nDATA lv_body TYPE string.\\nlv_body = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"object\\\":\\\"response\\\",\\\"output\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `,` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\"}\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `]}`.\\n```\\n\\nWait, inside backtick literal I can write single quotes freely. But `\\\\\\\"` inside backticks: backslash is literal in backtick strings? In ABAP, backtick-quoted string literals (not templates) have NO escape sequences \u2014 everything literal. So `\\\\\\\"` is backslash+quote. \\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```\\nDATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\ncl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls ) ).\\n```\\n\\nThen check call 1 name = write_source, id = c1, and arguments unescaped equals expected:\\n\\nexpected args1 = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2014 after unescape, the `\\\\n` becomes a real newline. Hmm \u2014 unescape converts `\\\\n` to newline. So expected args string contains a real newline. To build expected in the test I'd need a newline char. I could instead just assert on specific things:\\n\\n- assert call1-arguments CS `'{'.` (the literal source fragment with the brace) \\n- assert call1-arguments NS 'call_id' (no trailing junk)\\n- assert call2-name = 'activate', call2-id = 'c2'.\\n\\nFor a clean red test, asserting lines( lt_calls ) = 2 is the core. Plus argument purity: NS '\\\"status\\\"' and NS 'call_id'.\\n\\nBut for the green check after fix, I want to be sure the arguments are exactly right. I can build the expected string with a real newline: DATA lv_nl ... In ABAP, I can get newline via cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline. That's safe and standard. Let me use that for expected:\\n\\nexpected = '{\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.' \\u0026\\u0026 newline \\u0026\\u0026 'lv_open = ''{''.\\\"}'\\n\\nHmm, with '...' quotes I need doubled single quotes for the literal quote: 'lv_open = ''{''.' gives lv_open = '{'. Good.\\n\\nActually simpler: skip exact-match, use CS/NS assertions. But exact match is better. Let me use exact match with cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline.\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 does unescape turn `\\\\n` into |\\\\n| (whatever that is in this runtime)? If |\\\\n| in this runtime is a real newline char, then fine, matches cl_abap_char_utilities=\\u003enewline. UNESCAPE_CTRL test passes, suggesting unescape(`\\\\n`) equals whatever the test expected. Risky. I'll do both: exact-match assertion is nice-to-have; if it's flaky due to newline semantics, use CS instead. To be safe, I'll assert:\\n\\n- lines = 2 (core bug: second call lost)\\n- call2 name/id correct\\n- call1 arguments CS `'{'.` and NS 'call_id' and NS 'status' (purity)\\n\\nThat avoids newline semantics entirely. Good, keep it robust.\\n\\nAlso add a chat-format test for symmetry: chat body with tool_calls array, first arguments containing '{' literal, second call present.\\n\\nChat body:\\n\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"chatcmpl-1\\\",\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"index\\\":0,\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"DATA lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]},\\\"finish_reason\\\":\\\"tool_calls\\\"}]}\\n\\nparse_calls_chat: extract_balanced(body, 'tool_calls', '[', ']') \u2192 the array. Note the array contains braces inside the escaped string. The bracket scan would be fooled by `[` `]` inside string values too \u2014 but our test has none; brace is the reported bug. The entry splitter is brace-blind \u2192 with the fix, split works.\\n\\nHmm wait, one more subtlety for the chat path after fixing extract_balanced: extract_balanced scans for brackets; inside the arguments string there's no bracket, fine.\\n\\nBut also: in the entry splitter, the fix makes it string-aware. Good.\\n\\nNow also think: does the fix to extract_balanced break extract_str usage? No, extract_str is regex-based, unchanged.\\n\\nDoes the fix break other callers of extract_balanced? Callers: parse_calls_chat (tool_calls array), parse_calls_responses (output array), build_input_items (tool_calls array in stored content), zif chat (output array), and in tools/registry/other classes? Let me grep mentally \u2014 zcl_pia_00_registry maybe uses extract_balanced? I haven't read it. Changing extract_balanced to be string-aware only improves correctness. There may be tests in JSON_UTIL testclasses for extract_balanced (BALANCED_OBJECT, BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY, BALANCED_MISSING_KEY, BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET, BALANCED_UNCLOSED) \u2014 my change must keep those green. Let me consider each with the new implementation:\\n\\n- BALANCED_MISSING_KEY: key absent \u2192 FIND fails \u2192 RETURN initial. Same.\\n- BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET: key found but no open char \u2192 same.\\n- BALANCED_UNCLOSED: depth never returns to 0 \u2192 loop ends at last char. New impl: in-string at end \u2192 same result. Probably test expects the rest of string. New impl returns lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len) with lv_len = len-1-lv_start+1. Same.\\n- BALANCED_OBJECT / NESTED: standard \u2192 same.\\n\\nEdge: what if the value after key is not a string with braces... same behavior.\\n\\nOne subtle case in new impl: the open char itself could be inside a string before the real open. E.g. `\\\"key\\\": \\\"text [ real\\\" ...` \u2014 old code found first '[' inside the string. New code skips strings, finds real '['. Improvement.\\n\\nAlso the iv_open for objects is '{' \u2014 if key value is a string starting later... fine.\\n\\nNow, one more consideration: performance \u2014 string concat rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 char is O(n\u00B2) but strings here are small-ish (read_object outputs up to... source could be 60KB!). extract_balanced on a read_object result: the WHILE loop with rv_ building only at the end \u2014 fine, only substring extraction at end. But hmm, extract_balanced is called on the LLM response body, which contains tool results? No, tool results are in input items, not response. Response bodies are small. But the string-skipping loop itself is char-by-char with substring-free access via offsets \u2014 fine.\\n\\nWait, actually there's a performance trap: `iv_json+lv_off` in extract_balanced creates a copy \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAlso split_entries on large arrays \u2014 fine.\\n\\nNow \u2014 before writing, let me double check where else split_entries/extract_balanced matter for the *arguments unescape* in parse: ls-arguments = unescape(extract_balanced(lv_e, 'arguments', '{', '}')). For entry 1 (fixed), lv_e = {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{...}\\\"}. extract_balanced finds \\\"arguments\\\" then first '{' \u2014 which is inside the string value: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...`. Depth counting: '{' at start of string value \u2192 depth 1. Then '{' literal inside source (the '{' char) \u2192 depth 2. Then '}' closing source string's object \u2192 depth 1. Then `\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 the final '}' \u2192 depth 0. Total extracted = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"zcl_pia_demo\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\nlv_open = '{'.\\\"}`. unescape \u2192 correct. \\n\\nNow the red test. Also should I write a test that directly exercises extract_balanced with braces in strings? That would go in JSON_UTIL's testclasses include, which I can't read \u2014 I'd wipe 30 tests. Don't. Keep tests in LLM_HTTP testclasses (currently empty per run_tests).\\n\\nHmm, but wait: is it acceptable that the test lives in LLM_HTTP while the fix is in JSON_UTIL? Yes \u2014 bug reported at behavior level, test at parser level.\\n\\nAlso, maybe I should check the registry/other places where tool arguments with braces could break (e.g. invoke_call parses arguments JSON via extract_balanced?). Let me read zcl_pia_00_registry quickly to see if arguments parsing would also choke on unbalanced braces in string values. If registry uses split_entries/extract_balanced on arguments to get \\\"source\\\", the same bug would corrupt the write. The bug report says only the first call ran with junk appended \u2014 that's parse. But the tool result correctness matters too. Let me read registry.\\n\\nLet me first read ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_2026100719405386fce695a51c4ddc\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7227985825189847949\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7227985825189847949\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":8776,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":704},\"output_tokens\":4114,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":4096},\"total_tokens\":12890}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373325,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007194159cbc2fe0b3d514d82\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now let's look at the testclasses of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL to know what tests exist and where to add. LLM_HTTP has no local tests (0 methods). JSON_UTIL has LTCL_JSON_UTIL with 30 methods. I'll add tests to JSON_UTIL for split_entries and extract_balanced string-awareness, and maybe tests in LLM_HTTP for parse_calls_chat.\\n\\nLet me read the testclasses of JSON_UTIL to write the new full include. I need the full source to preserve existing tests.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007194159cbc2fe0b3d514d82\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011663713101079\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011663713101079\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9419,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":896},\"output_tokens\":134,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":116},\"total_tokens\":9553}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373569,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_2026100719420667b6e873ba8f458a\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now let me write the reproducing test first. I'll add tests to ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (testclasses include \u2014 currently empty/nonexistent; need to write full source).\\n\\nI need to write the testclasses include for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. Since there are no existing test classes there, I'll create one with tests:\\n\\n1. `RESPONSES_BRACE_IN_ARGS` \u2014 the bug: responses body with two calls, first args contain `'{'.` escaped as `{`. Verify 2 calls parsed, first args correct.\\n2. `CHAT_BRACE_IN_ARGS` \u2014 same for chat parse.\\n\\nWait \u2014 is the chat parser also buggy in a similar way? In chat, arguments is a string; the `{` is escaped, so extract_balanced from `\\\"arguments\\\"` won't be confused... Let me check chat path with the analogous body:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{' . ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}\\n```\\n\\nextract_balanced(json=body, key='tool_calls', open='[', close=']'): finds key, then '[', depth 1, scans: the escaped `\\\\\\\"{` ... `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \u2014 hmm in the actual body string, the escaped quote is `\\\\\\\"`. Scanning characters: `{` inside the escaped arguments appears as `{` (unescaped char). Yes it's counted. Same bug. So both parsers affected.\\n\\nActually, for the chat parser, does the bug matter the same way? The first entry's `arguments` extract_balanced would end early, leaving trailing text `\\\",\\\"status\\\":...` \u2014 wait chat entries don't have status. For chat: entry2 = `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 split_entries starts at lv_pos after first entry. Where does the first entry end? At the `}` closing \\\"arguments\\\" string early. Then rest of entry 1 `\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},` contains no `{`... then `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\"...` \u2014 found. So second entry IS parsed, but its `arguments` may be OK. But the first entry's arguments has trailing junk. And also entry1's remaining text `\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...` \u2014 wait, after early end at `{`+`}`, the rest of entry1 is `}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]`. split_entries continues: FIND `{` from lv_pos \u2192 finds `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\"`? Hmm wait, the remaining text after the early-closed first entry: everything after the `'{'`... let me carefully write it.\\n\\nEntry1 raw: {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"\\\"}\\n\\nThe early `}` in `{'.` \u2014 split_entries counts depth: `{` at start (depth1), then scans... reaches `{` inside args \u2192 depth 2, then `}` right after \u2192 depth 1... continues, then `}` closing \\\"arguments\\\" string \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 entry = {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...lv_open = '{'.  ...\\\\\\\"}. Hmm wait no: the first `}` after `{` is the one in `{'.` \u2014 that sets depth 1\u2192... let me re-trace: depth starts at 1 after opening `{`. At `{` (inside args): depth 2. At `}` (in `{'.`): depth 1. Then later `}` (closing the arguments JSON before the quote): depth 0 \u2192 END. So entry1 = from start to that `}` \u2014 which actually is the FULL correct entry! Because the `{` from `{'.` and the `}` from args-end cancel out!\\n\\nOh interesting. So in THIS specific case (exactly one unmatched `{`), split_entries gets entry1 right (the `{`/`}` pair inside args... wait no. Let's count all braces in the args string: `{` at args start (unescaped? no\u2014args string content is `{\\\"name\\\":...}`, so chars: `{`, `\\\"name\\\"`, ... `}`). Inside source there is `'{'.` \u2014 one extra `{`. So braces in entry1: entry start `{`, args `{`, source `{`, args end `}`, entry end `}`. Balanced count: 4 opens, 3 closes... wait: entry `{` + args `{` + source `{` = 3 opens; closes: args `}` + entry `}` = 2 closes. So depth: 1 \u2192 2 (args `{`) \u2192 3 (source `{`) \u2192 2 (args `}`) \u2192 1 (entry `}`)... then continue scanning into entry 2! Entry1's depth never hits 0 inside entry1. Next `{` in entry2 (`{` of entry2 start) \u2192 depth 2, `\\\"name\\\"`..., `arguments` `{` \u2192 3, args `}` \u2192 2, entry2 `}` \u2192 1... then `]` ... then continues to outer structure: `\\\"output\\\":[...]` was already consumed; after the array comes `},\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\"...}` \u2192 `}` \u2192 depth 0.\\n\\nSo entry1 = everything up to that trailing `}` \u2014 matches the bug report: arguments arrive with extra text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})` \u2014 hmm wait, why does the report show arguments containing `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`? Let's look at how arguments were extracted in parse_calls_responses:\\n\\n```\\nls-arguments = unescape( extract_balanced( json = lv_e key = 'arguments' open = '{' close = '}' ) )\\n```\\n\\nlv_e = entry1 (which spans beyond the true entry, including entry2 and trailing response text up to a `}`).\\n\\nextract_balanced finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` (first occurrence), then first `{` after it \u2014 that's the args `{`. Then scans: `{` from source \u2192 depth 2... then args `}` \u2192 depth 1... then continues past the closing quote! Scanning for `}`: entry1's own closing `}` \u2192 depth 0. So arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}` + `\\\"` \u2014 hmm. Actually the raw substring from args `{` to entry1's `}` includes the closing quote of the arguments string. Then unescape removes escapes. So arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}` + trailing `\\\"`? Hmm, that gives arguments with an extra quote at end, not `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":...`.\\n\\nWait, the report says: arguments arrive with extra text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Hmm \u2014 with call_id c1 \u2014 that's entry1's own call_id! That means extract_balanced ran over a string where after the args... hmm, unless the order of keys is different. Maybe in the actual response the keys are ordered: `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{...}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 i.e., call_id AFTER arguments! Yes, that's typical of the responses API. So entry1 = {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}.\\n\\nNow: split_entries \u2014 braces: entry `{`, args `{`, source `{` (extra), args `}`, entry `}`. Depth: 1,2,3,2,1 \u2192 after entry `}` depth is 1, NOT 0. So scanning continues into `,{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\"...}`: entry2 `{` \u2192 2, args `{` \u2192 3, args `}` \u2192 2, entry2 `}` \u2192 1... then `]` then `}` (closing \\\"output\\\" array's parent? no\u2014the `]` then `,` then `\\\"id\\\":...` then final `}`) \u2192 0. Entry1 spans BOTH entries + trailing `\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` etc.\\n\\nThen extract_balanced(json=entry1, key='arguments'): finds `\\\"arguments\\\"`, first `{` = args start, scans: source `{` \u2192 2, args `}` \u2192 1, continues... where's the next `}`? After args close quote: `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 that `}` \u2192 depth 0. So arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}` + `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 EXACTLY the reported symptom: extra text after the JSON!\\n\\nAnd entry2: after entry1 (ending at the response's final `}`), scanning continues from there \u2014 nothing left \u2192 only ONE call. Matches \\\"the second call is lost\\\".\\n\\nSo the root cause is definitively: `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` count braces/brackets without skipping JSON string literals.\\n\\nNow the fix. Minimal, contained in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL:\\n\\n1. `extract_balanced`: when scanning for the opening and for the matching close, skip over `\\\"...\\\"` strings (respecting `\\\\\\\"`).\\n2. `split_entries`: same string-skipping when finding `{` starts and matching closes.\\n3. Also `extract_str` uses PCRE with `[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.` \u2014 that's already string-aware. Fine.\\n\\nAlso for chat parser: arguments `{` inside string is escaped so key search fine, but balanced scan same issue. Fixed by the same util change.\\n\\nAdditionally, in parse_calls_responses the split is on entries; with fix, entries are correct.\\n\\nLet me now write the fix in extract_balanced:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n  FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  DATA(lv_rest) = iv_json+lv_off.\\n  DATA(lv_len) = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n  \\\" find the opening delimiter, ignoring delimiters inside string values\\n  scan_for( ... )\\n```\\n\\nI'll implement a small private helper? The class is PUBLIC FINAL; I can add PRIVATE SECTION methods. Let me restructure:\\n\\nAdd private class-methods:\\n- `skip_string` importing iv_text type string iv_pos type i returning value(rv_) type i \u2014 returns index of the closing quote (scanning from a quote at iv_pos).\\n- Or a single helper `scan_balanced` that handles finding open and matching close with string-awareness.\\n\\nImplementation of extract_balanced:\\n\\n```\\nDATA lv_i TYPE i.\\nDATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool.\\n\\\" find key\\n...\\n\\\" from lv_off, scan to find iv_open outside strings\\nlv_i = 0. lv_in_str = abap_false.\\nDATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\nWHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n  lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n  IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n    IF lv_ch = `\\\\`. lv_i = lv_i + 2. CONTINUE. ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_false. ENDIF.\\n  ELSE.\\n    IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n    ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open. lv_start = lv_i. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\nENDWHILE.\\nIF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nCareful: `lv_i = lv_i + 2. CONTINUE.` \u2014 inside WHILE with lv_i increment... using CONTINUE skips the rest, and I do the +2 manually. OK. Edge: backslash as last char: lv_i+2 beyond length \u2192 loop ends. Fine.\\n\\nThen match close from lv_start+1 with depth tracking, string-aware:\\n\\n```\\nlv_depth = 1.\\nlv_pos = lv_start.\\nWHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n  lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n  IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n    IF lv_ch = `\\\\. ... skip\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nCombine into one loop:\\n\\n```\\nlv_i = lv_start.\\nlv_in_str = abap_false.\\nlv_depth = 0.\\nWHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n  lv_ch = substring...\\n  IF lv_in_str...\\n```\\n\\nLet me write one unified scanner:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  DATA(lv_rest) = iv_json+lv_off.\\n  DATA(lv_len) = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n  DATA lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n  DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n  WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n    lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n      CONTINUE.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n      lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n      CONTINUE.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n      lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      IF lv_start \\u003c 0.\\n        lv_start = lv_i.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n      lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      IF lv_depth \\u003c= 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n        \\\" found the matching close of the first value that opens after the key\\n        lv_len2 = lv_i - lv_start + 1\\n        rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(...).\\n        RETURN.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n```\\n\\nWait, subtlety: for the `arguments` case, key = `\\\"arguments\\\"`, and the value is a STRING starting with `{` (escaped content). The scanning after key: `\\\":\\\"` then chars `\\\\\\\"` `{` `\\\\\\\"` ... \u2014 the value is a quoted string! `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. My scanner sees `\\\"` after colon \u2192 enters string mode \u2192 skips the whole args string including the `{` chars \u2192 never finds iv_open `{` outside a string! That breaks the chat/responses argument extraction entirely, since arguments is a JSON string whose content is JSON.\\n\\nHmm! That's the crux: the arguments value is a string containing JSON. So a purely string-aware scanner won't find the `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\":`. That's exactly why the original code works \\\"well enough\\\": it searches for the first `{` after the key \u2014 which is the first char of the escaped JSON (the content), and then must scan the STRING content as JSON (where braces appear as raw `{`/`}` chars and quotes appear as `\\\\\\\"`).\\n\\nSo the semantics needed: for `arguments`, we scan the escaped-JSON content. In the escaped content, quotes are `\\\\\\\"`, so a quote char that starts a JSON string is `\\\\\\\"` (backslash-quote), and a quote that ENDS it is also `\\\\\\\"`... Hmm, in the escaped content: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\"}` escaped is `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL\\\\\\\"}`. The quotes appear as `\\\\\\\"` each. So within the content, string boundaries are `\\\\\\\"` pairs \u2014 every quote is `\\\\\\\"`, there's no way to distinguish open/close except by parsing: a quote starts or ends a string alternating. Since escaped content has no unescaped quotes, a simple state machine: outside string, `\\\\\\\"` enters string; inside string, `\\\\\\\"` exits. But `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` (escaped backslash then quote)... in escaped content, an escaped backslash in the original becomes `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`, and escaped quote `\\\\\\\"`. So sequences: `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` = literal backslash, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` = literal backslash then string boundary quote. Parsing pairs of backslashes from the position works.\\n\\nSo a correct scanner over the escaped content: process backslash PAIRS. Outside string: `{`/`}`/`[`/`]` structural; `\\\\\\\"` toggles into string. Inside string: `\\\\\\\"` toggles out; `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` literal.\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 for arguments, the whole thing sits inside a JSON string in the body. The scanner starts AFTER the opening `\\\"` of that string (extract_balanced finds first `{` after key). So the scan region is the escaped content until the closing `\\\"` of the outer string \u2014 which appears as an unescaped `\\\"`. In the content, `\\\\\\\"` never... hmm: the closing quote of the arguments string is a bare `\\\"` (not preceded by an odd number of backslashes). E.g. `...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"...`: chars: `\\\\` `\\\"` `}` `\\\"` `,` \u2014 the args content `{...\\\\\\\"}` ends, then bare `\\\"` closes the string.\\n\\nSo a scanner that handles both levels: treat `\\\\X` as a literal two-char unit (skip 2); treat bare `\\\"` as string toggle; count braces only outside strings. Let's verify with the args case:\\n\\nStart at `{` (depth 1, outside string since we treat `\\\\\\\"`-escaped quotes... hmm wait). Scan: `{` depth1. `\\\\` \u2192 skip 2 (skips `\\\\\\\"`). `name` ... `\\\\` skip 2 (`\\\\\\\"`). `:` ... `\\\\` skip2 ... `ZCL` ... `\\\\` skip2 ... `}` depth 0 \u2192 close found.\\n\\nNow the bug case: content contains `'{'.` \u2014 raw `{` inside the source string within the args content. In the escaped content, the source is a string in the inner JSON, so the quotes around it are `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`, and the `{` inside is a bare `{` char (braces need no escaping). So my scanner: at `\\\\'` (escaped single quote) skip2... then `{` \u2014 but we're INSIDE the inner string! Because the scanner toggled into string mode at the `\\\\\\\"` that opened the source string. So `{` inside is skipped. Then `\\\\'` skip2, `.`, then `\\\\` skip2 (`\\\\\\\"` closing source string) \u2192 exits string. Then continue: `\\\\n` etc. Then final `\\\\` skip2, `}` depth 0 \u2192 correct end!\\n\\nThe key: quotes preceded by a backslash are literals (or toggles in inner level). Hmm wait \u2014 I said treat `\\\\X` as skip-2 unit, and bare `\\\"` as toggle. In inner JSON, string boundaries ARE `\\\\\\\"` (escaped quotes). If I skip them as units, then `{` inside the inner string is treated as structural! Let me re-examine.\\n\\nTwo-level structure:\\n- Outer: arguments value is a JSON string: `\\\"{\\\"escaped content\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 hmm no: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\"`. The content between the outer quotes is the escaped inner JSON.\\n- Inner (escaped): `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ...\\\"}` where each `\\\"` is written `\\\\\\\"`.\\n\\nA scanner that just skips `\\\\X` pairs and treats bare `\\\"` as toggles would see NO quotes at all in the inner content (all are `\\\\\\\"`), so everything is \\\"outside string\\\" and every `{`/`}` counts \u2014 including the one in `'{'.` \u2192 same bug.\\n\\nA scanner that treats `\\\\\\\"` as a string toggle (alternating) gets the inner structure right: `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` \u2192 toggle on at `\\\\`+`\\\"`, off at `\\\\`+`\\\"`. And braces inside toggled regions are skipped. But then it must also not be confused by `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` or by the OUTER closing quote.\\n\\nWhich semantics do we need for which call site?\\n- `extract_balanced(body, 'output', '[', ']')` \u2014 the array is real outer-level JSON; entries are real objects; strings inside are bare-quoted with `\\\\\\\"` for escaped quotes. Scanner: bare `\\\"` toggles, `\\\\X` skip 2, braces/brackets structural outside strings. \u2713 correct.\\n- `extract_balanced(body, 'tool_calls', '[', ']')` \u2014 same. \u2713\\n- `extract_balanced(entry, 'arguments', '{', '}')` \u2014 here the value is a STRING containing escaped JSON. The first `{` after the key is inside that string (the content's first char). The matching `}` is the content's last char. Scanning with outer semantics: bare `\\\"` toggles \u2192 the outer opening quote of the args string toggles ON before we reach `{`... but we START at the `{` which is after the opening quote \u2014 we're inside the outer string already but the scanner doesn't know. Treating `\\\\\\\"` as skip-2, the inner string quotes vanish \u2192 all braces structural \u2192 bug remains for `'{'.`.\\n\\nHmm. So to fix the arguments extraction, the scanner must, after the key, ENTER the string and parse the escaped inner JSON with `\\\\\\\"`-toggle semantics.\\n\\nApproach: make extract_balanced smarter: after finding the key, look at what follows: `\\\":` then optional ws then either iv_open directly (real object) or `\\\"` (string). If it starts with `\\\"`, we are in the string case: start after that quote, and scan the escaped content with toggle-on-`\\\\\\\"` semantics until depth 0, where quotes toggle as `\\\\\\\"` pairs (i.e., each `\\\\\\\"` toggles string state in the inner JSON), and `\\\\X` other than quote is literal (skip appropriately), and a bare `\\\"` ends the outer string (should not be crossed... if depth hits 0 before, fine).\\n\\nLet me design the inner scanner:\\n- state: in_inner_string (bool), depth.\\n- iterate chars:\\n  - if char = `\\\\`: look at next char: if next = `\\\"` \u2192 toggle in_inner_string. Advance 2 (whether toggle or literal, both consume 2). Hmm: `\\\\\\\\` (escaped backslash in inner = `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` outer... in the escaped content, a literal backslash of the original source appears as `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`). Processing char by char: at first `\\\\`, next is `\\\\` \u2192 literal, advance 2 \u2192 skips both. Good. At `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`: this is an inner string boundary (open or close) \u2192 toggle. Good. Other `\\\\n`, `\\\\\\\"`... wait `\\\\\\\"` is quote; `\\\\n` literal newline-escape \u2192 advance 2, stays non-string/inside string as before. Good.\\n  - else if char = `\\\"` (bare): that's the END of the outer string (the arguments value ended). If we reach it with depth \\u003e 0 \u2192 malformed; just stop there (defensive: return what we have). Actually with the bug-free input we stop at depth 0 before it.\\n  - else: structural char if not in_inner_string: `{`/`[` increase, `}`/`]` decrease.\\n\\nHmm, but there's a subtlety with `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` when inside... e.g. inner string content containing an escaped quote: original source has `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 in inner JSON it's `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \u2192 in outer escaped content: `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` becomes... let me be careful with levels:\\n\\nLevel 0: actual ABAP source text: `lv_s = '\\\"'.`\\nLevel 1 (the arguments JSON string content \u2014 \\\"inner JSON\\\" text): the source is a string in the inner JSON: `\\\"lv_s = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\"` \u2014 i.e., quotes escaped: `\\\\\\\"`, backslash escaped: `\\\\\\\\`.\\nLevel 2 (arguments value embedded in the outer response JSON): every `\\\"` \u2192 `\\\\\\\"`, every `\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\\\\\`.\\n\\nSo level-2 text for `lv_s = '\\\"'`: level1 = `lv_s = '\\\\\\\"'.` \u2192 level2 = `lv_s = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'.` \u2014 chars: `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\"` `'` `.`. My scanner at the first `\\\\`: next is `\\\\` \u2192 literal, advance 2. Then at `\\\\`: next is `\\\"` \u2192 toggle. If we were inside a string (we are \u2014 this is content of the source string in inner JSON), toggling OUT. Then `'`, `.` \u2014 outside string... then next `\\\\\\\"` toggles back in. Hmm \u2014 that misparse could matter if there were a brace between. In `'\\\\''`-style edge cases, alternating toggles can drift. But: the drift is only about which chars are considered structural. Between the toggle-out and toggle-back-in there are `'` and `.` \u2014 no braces \u2192 harmless. Drift errors only matter if structural chars appear in the mis-attributed region. Perfect robustness would require counting consecutive backslashes: a `\\\"` preceded by an ODD number of backslashes is a boundary; even \u2192 literal. Let me implement it properly with a backslash-run counter \u2014 not much harder:\\n\\nFor the escaped-inner scan:\\n```\\nrun = 0 (number of consecutive backslashes ending at previous position... )\\n```\\nSimplest: iterate; when char = `\\\\`, count the full run of backslashes n; if n odd, then the quote after is a boundary: toggle, advance n+1. If even: the run is literals followed by a non-boundary quote?? no \u2014 even run then a quote means the quote is... hmm: even number of backslashes = pairs of literal backslashes, so the following `\\\"` is a boundary TOO? No wait: in level-2, a literal backslash is `\\\\\\\\` (2 chars), a boundary quote is `\\\\\\\"` (1 backslash + quote). So a run of n backslashes followed by `\\\"`: n/2 literal backslashes if n even \u2192 quote is boundary; (n-1)/2 literals + boundary-escaped... hmm no. Level-2 encodes level-1 content. In level-1, `\\\\\\\"` (backslash+quote) is an escaped quote INSIDE a string (literal `\\\"`), and a bare `\\\"` is a boundary. Encoding to level 2: `\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\`, `\\\"`\u2192`\\\\\\\"`. So:\\n- level1 escaped-quote `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 level2: `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` (3 backslashes? no: `\\\\\\\\` then `\\\\\\\"` = chars `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"` = 3 backslashes + quote). So odd run (3) + quote = literal quote in level 1 content.\\n- level1 boundary `\\\"` \u2192 level2 `\\\\\\\"` = 1 backslash + quote \u2192 odd run(1) + quote = boundary.\\n\\nHmm so both odd?! Because escaping doubles backslashes each level: level1 `\\\\\\\"`(1 bs) \u2192 level2 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` (2+1=3 bs). Level1 `\\\"`\u2192 level2 `\\\\\\\"` (1 bs). Both odd runs. And even runs: level1 `\\\\\\\\\\\"` (literal bs + boundary quote: 2... hmm level1 `\\\\\\\\` is escaped backslash (literal), then `\\\"` boundary \u2192 level2: `\\\\\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`, `\\\"`\u2192`\\\\\\\"` = 4+1=5 backslashes + quote. Always odd!\\n\\nRight \u2014 of course: each level-1 backslash becomes 2 level-2 backslashes, plus 1 if the quote is escaped. So in level 2, a quote is a boundary iff preceded by an odd run of backslashes... but ALL quotes have odd runs. Wait: level1 boundary quote \u2192 level2 `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 the quote is escaped at level 2 (because it's inside the level-2 string!). Ugh, yes: EVERYTHING inside the arguments string is escaped at level 2. So at level 2, you cannot distinguish by backslash count alone \u2014 you need to know level-1 structure.\\n\\nOK so the right way: decode level 2 \u2192 level 1 progressively while parsing level 1. I.e., a small JSON-string-aware scanner operating on the ESCAPED text where the escape sequences are: `\\\\\\\"` = literal quote... no wait. Hmm, I conflated. Let me redo.\\n\\nThe outer response JSON contains: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` where `...` is the level-2 escaped form. If I UNESCAPE the outer string, I get level-1: the actual arguments JSON text, e.g. `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ...\\\"}`. In level-1, strings are `\\\"...\\\"` with `\\\\\\\"` escapes and `\\\\\\\\` escapes.\\n\\nSo the clean fix for extract_balanced on `arguments`: after the key, if the value starts with `\\\"`, take the string value (using existing string-aware scan: bare quotes with escape pairs \u2014 that's exactly what a proper outer-level string scanner does), unescape it, THEN extract the balanced `{...}` from the unescaped text with the normal string-aware scanner. Two-phase.\\n\\nBut the current code architecture: extract_balanced returns the raw balanced text and callers unescape after. In parse_calls_*: `ls-arguments = unescape( extract_balanced( entry, 'arguments', '{','}' ) )`.\\n\\nOption A (clean, no util change): change callers to first extract the arguments STRING via a new util method `extract_json_string_value` \u2014 hmm, but extract_str already does that with PCRE `'\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"'` \u2014 string-aware PCRE! So `extract_str( entry, 'arguments' )` returns the escaped inner JSON correctly, then `unescape` it, then `extract_balanced(unescaped, 'arguments', '{', '}')`? Hmm, in the unescaped text, find key `arguments`? The unescaped args text is `{\\\"name\\\":...}` \u2014 no `\\\"arguments\\\"` key inside. But extract_balanced requires the key... I could pass the text with a prefix, or better: add an optional iv_key = '' handling: if iv_key initial, start at offset 0. Or simpler: use a variant `extract_balanced_from( iv_json, iv_start... )`. \\n\\nSimplest: extend extract_balanced: if the first non-ws char after the key+colon is `\\\"`, then extract the string value (string-aware), unescape it, and continue the balanced scan within that unescaped text from position 0. Then the returned rv_ is the balanced substring of the UNESCAPED inner JSON. Callers currently do `unescape( extract_balanced(...) )`. unescape of already-unescaped text with backslashes... The source code contains `\\\\` chars? ABAP source might contain backslashes rarely; unescape would mangle `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\`. Hmm, risky.\\n\\nOption B: keep returning the ESCAPED form as today (so `unescape(...)` in callers stays), but make the depth scan string-aware AT THE ESCAPED LEVEL with proper semantics. As established, at the escaped level the inner JSON quotes are `\\\\\\\"` and inner escaped quotes are `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, inner escaped backslash `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`. A scanner over escaped text: quote boundary = `\\\"` char; to know if it's a boundary or literal we track escapes: scan pairs: when we see `\\\\`, consume 2 chars (`\\\\X`). Then a `\\\\\\\"` sequence is consumed as a unit \u2014 but we NEED it to toggle string state (since it represents a level-1 boundary quote). And `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\`+`\\\\` consumed as unit, then `\\\\\\\"` toggles. And `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"`... wait `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\` = two units. So: consume `\\\\X` units; each `\\\\\\\"` unit toggles string state; bare `\\\"` (not preceded by backslash) = end of the outer string (arguments value) \u2014 with correct input, depth reaches 0 before it.\\n\\nCheck with bug case, escaped args content (level 2): `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}`\\nScan: `{` depth1 (outside string). `\\\\\\\"` toggle \u2192 in string. `name` ... `\\\\\\\"` toggle out. `:` `\\\\\\\"` in. `ZCL` `\\\\\\\"` out. `,` `\\\\\\\"` in. `source` `\\\\\\\"` out. `:` `\\\\\\\"` in. `CLASS...` \u2014 inside string, `{` of `'{'.` skipped \u2713. ... `\\\\\\\"` out. `}` depth 0 \u2713 \u2192 returns escaped content `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 callers unescape it \u2192 correct arguments!\\n\\nAnd what about a source containing `\\\\\\\"`? Level-1 source string: `lv = '\\\\\\\"'.` \u2192 level-1 JSON: `\\\\\\\"lv = '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'.\\\\\\\"`... ugh levels again. Level1 args JSON contains source as `\\\"lv = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\"`. Level2 escapes every `\\\\`\u2192`\\\\\\\\`,`\\\"`\u2192`\\\\\\\"`: \u2192 `\\\\\\\"lv = '\\\\\\\\''...` hmm: level1 `\\\\\\\"` (escaped quote in source) \u2192 level2 `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`. Scanner: `\\\\`+`\\\\` unit (literal, no toggle), then `\\\\`+`\\\"` unit \u2192 toggle. Correct: that's an escaped quote INSIDE the level-1 source string, so toggling would be WRONG... wait: level1 text: `\\\"lv = '\\\\\\\"'.\\\"` \u2014 chars: `\\\"` b g s ... `\\\\` `\\\"` `'` `.` `\\\"`. The `\\\\\\\"` here is an escaped quote (literal), NOT a boundary. At level 2 this becomes `\\\\\\\"lv = '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'.\\\\\\\"`: chars: `\\\\\\\"` (boundary start), `lv = `, `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\"` `'` `.` `\\\\\\\"` (boundary end). Scanner: `\\\\\\\"` toggle in. `lv = `. Then `\\\\`+`\\\\` unit literal. Then `\\\\`+`\\\"` unit \u2192 toggles OUT (wrong! it's a literal quote inside the string). Then `'` `.` outside. Then `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 toggles IN. Net effect: state correct again at the end, but between the two mis-toggles the scanner thinks it's outside a string. If a `{` or `}` appeared there (e.g., source `lv = '\\\\\\\"{'`), it would miscount. This is the classic double-escape ambiguity \u2014 cannot be resolved by a simple scanner without proper recursive unescaping.\\n\\nHow much do we care? The bug report is about a single unbalanced `{`. Perfect handling of `\\\\\\\"` inside escaped strings is a deeper rabbit hole (proper fix = full JSON parse). I'll implement the pragmatic scanner that handles: strings at the current level (bare quotes toggling, `\\\\X` skip) AND the arguments-string case (`\\\\\\\"` toggles). Hmm \u2014 but these two conflict: at outer level, `\\\\\\\"` is an escaped quote (literal, no toggle), while in the args case `\\\\\\\"` IS the level-1 boundary. The SAME character sequence means different things depending on level. A single scanner can't do both.\\n\\nResolution: handle the two cases separately in extract_balanced:\\n- Case 1 (value is a real object/array): scan with outer semantics (bare `\\\"` toggles, `\\\\X` skips as unit).\\n- Case 2 (value is a string starting with `{`/`[`): the first `{` after the key is INSIDE the string \u2014 as today. Then scan the escaped content with LEVEL-1 semantics where `\\\\\\\"` toggles and `\\\\\\\\` is a unit... and bare `\\\"` ends. But as shown, level-1-escaped text scanned with `\\\\\\\"`-toggle semantics mis-handles `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` sequences... let me check: in level-1 semantics, strings are `\\\"...\\\"`, escapes `\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\`. So a proper level-1 scanner: bare `\\\"` toggles; `\\\\X` skip-2. The level-2 text is NOT level-1 text though. Argh.\\n\\nOK, cleanest correct approach: Option A \u2014 for the string case, take the full string value using outer-level string scanning (which `extract_str`'s PCRE already does correctly: `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)`), then UNESCAPE it to get true level-1 text, then run the balanced scan on THAT with proper string-aware semantics, and return the balanced substring of the level-1 text. The callers currently do `unescape(extract_balanced(...))` \u2014 unescaping twice? If extract_balanced returns level-1 text (already unescaped once), then the caller's unescape would corrupt literal backslashes in source (`\\\\\\\\`\u2192`\\\\`, `\\\\\\\"`\u2192`\\\"`)... Actually what does the FINAL desired `ls-arguments` contain? The arguments JSON as true text (level 1): `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}`. That's level-1. So: extract_balanced should return level-1 balanced text, and callers should NOT unescape. I can change both util and the two callers in llm_http (they're my code). Also check other callers of extract_balanced with string values... In llm_http: arguments (both parsers) \u2014 those are the string-case. Others ('output', 'tool_calls', 'messages', 'function') are real-object cases. In session from_json: 'messages' real. So only arguments is the string case.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. `extract_balanced` becomes string-aware (outer level): skip string contents when finding/matching delimiters. If the value after the key is a string (starts with `\\\"`), then: find the first iv_open... hmm \u2014 implement: after key+colon, skip ws; if next char = `\\\"`: take string value via string-aware scan; rv_ = balanced-scan( unescape(stringvalue) ) \u2014 recursive call with a marker to avoid infinite recursion: the unescaped text starts with `{`, so scanning for first `{` from position 0... but extract_balanced requires a key. Add a branch: if iv_key is initial \u2192 start scan at offset 0 of iv_json. Recursive call with iv_key = `` on the unescaped text. But callers pass 'arguments' as key... So: `rv_ = extract_balanced( iv_json = unescape(lv_strval) iv_key = '' ... )`. And the top of the method: if iv_key IS INITIAL \u2192 lv_off = 0. Good.\\n\\nBut then callers do `unescape(extract_balanced(...))` \u2014 must remove the outer unescape for arguments. In parse_calls_chat and parse_calls_responses: `ls-arguments = extract_balanced(...)` directly (already unescaped inside). \\n\\nHold on \u2014 for the string-case, does the returned level-1 text keep inner escapes? Yes: level-1 text has `\\\\\\\"` for quotes inside the source etc. The final `ls-arguments` = level-1 JSON text \u2014 exactly what tools expect (they parse it with extract_str etc. \u2014 e.g. read the source param: `extract_str(args,'source')` then `unescape` \u2192 real source). Today's behavior post-unescape: currently `unescape(extract_balanced(...))` where extract_balanced returned level-2-ish text \u2192 unescape \u2192 level-1. Same final result. \u2713 So with my change, final arguments text is the same as today (in the non-buggy case) \u2014 good, no downstream breakage.\\n\\nLet me double check the non-buggy flows to avoid regressions:\\n\\nparse_calls_chat entry: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}`.\\n- New extract_balanced(entry, 'arguments', '{','}'): finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` at offset; after colon skip ws; next char = `\\\"` \u2192 string case: scan string value string-aware: from the opening `\\\"`, content until closing bare `\\\"` handling `\\\\X`: content = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}`, unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Z\\\"}`, recursive balanced from offset 0: first `{` depth 1... scanning with string-awareness: `\\\"name\\\"` string skipped, `:` , `\\\"Z\\\"` skipped, `}` depth 0 \u2192 returns `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Z\\\"}`. \u2713\\n- Old behavior: extract_balanced returned `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\"}` then unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Z\\\"}`. Same. \u2713\\n\\nChat body 'tool_calls' array: extract_balanced(body,'tool_calls','[',']') \u2014 real array. New string-aware scan: e.g. body has `content\\\":null` before tool_calls; scanning from key: first `[` outside strings \u2713; depth matching skips strings like `\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_1\\\"` \u2713 and the arguments strings entirely (their `{`/`}` never counted) \u2713. End at matching `]` \u2713. Note: with string-awareness, the matching `]` is found BEFORE the args strings' braces... wait \u2014 args strings contain no `[`/`]` unescaped... the source param could contain `[` `]` inside the args string \u2014 string-aware scan skips them \u2713 (previously would miscount too!). Good.\\n\\nWait, one problem: in the string case for arguments \u2014 old code searched for the first `{` after the key which was the args content start. New code detects value-is-string. But what if value is `null` or a number (some models send arguments:\\\"{}\\\" always for function tools; type function requires string) \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAlso `BALANCED_*` existing tests in json_util testclasses: they call extract_balanced \u2014 must keep passing. Let me look at the existing test include of json_util to see them, and then write my changes + new tests. I need to read the testclasses include \u2014 read_object returns main include only? It says \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation)\\\". Test classes are in a separate include. Hmm \u2014 how do I read the existing testclasses include? The tool only reads main. But run_tests showed LTCL_JSON_UTIL with test names like BALANCED_OBJECT etc. To preserve them when writing the testclasses include, I need their source... The write_source tool says provide FULL source. If I can't read the testclasses include, I'd overwrite and lose existing tests!\\n\\nLet me try read_object \u2014 maybe it returns both includes? It returned only main. Hmm. Risk: writing testclasses for ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL would destroy the 30 existing tests. The task says \\\"One existing test, ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR, is already red... leave it as it is\\\" \u2014 implying tests must be preserved.\\n\\nWhere should the reproducing test live? The bug is in parsing (llm_http parse_calls_responses / json_util). The llm_http class has NO test classes yet (\\\"GREEN tests ran: classes 0\\\") \u2014 safe to create testclasses there without destroying anything. \\n\\nBut my fix also touches json_util (split_entries + extract_balanced + maybe unescape untouched). If I add tests to json_util's testclasses include, I'd overwrite existing ones since I can't read them. So: put ALL new tests in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP testclasses (testing parse_calls_responses/parse_calls_chat with the brace case, which exercises json_util indirectly). That avoids touching json_util's test include. And ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR in json_util stays untouched \u2014 it stays red as told. \\n\\nBut wait \u2014 can I still modify json_util's MAIN include without touching its testclasses? Yes \u2014 write_source(include='main') only rewrites main. The test classes remain. \\n\\nNow, also check split_entries fix (string-aware). Used for: output array entries, tool_calls entries, messages array, tools flattening. Fix with string-aware scanning:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA: lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0, lv_depth TYPE i, lv_start TYPE i,\\n        lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool, lv_ch TYPE string.\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  lv_len = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    \\\" find next '{' outside strings\\n    lv_start = -1.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n      lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_pos(1).\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\`. lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. CONTINUE. ENDIF.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_false. ENDIF.\\n      ELSE.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'. lv_start = lv_pos. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 subtlety: entries' string contents include ESCAPED quotes `\\\\\\\"`. With outer-level semantics (bare `\\\"` toggles, `\\\\X` skip), the args string `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`: opening bare `\\\"` toggles in; `\\\\\\\"` units skipped; closing bare `\\\"` toggles out. \u2713 Braces inside skipped \u2713.\\n\\nBUT: the args string contains the level-2-escaped source which may contain `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` sequences... outer-level semantics handles any `\\\\X` pairs generically \u2014 a bare `\\\"` only appears at true string boundaries at THAT level. But careful: `\\\\` at end consumed 2 might skip a bare `\\\"`?? E.g. content `...\\\\\\\\` then closing `\\\"`: chars `\\\\` `\\\\` `\\\"` \u2014 at first `\\\\`, skip 2 (both backslashes), then `\\\"` \u2192 toggle out \u2713. And `...\\\\\\\"` before closing: `\\\\` `\\\"` then `\\\"`?? that'd be escaped quote then close: skip2 then toggle \u2713. Correct because at any level, escapes come in `\\\\X` pairs. \u2713\\n\\nNow: with split_entries fixed (string-aware), the output array splits correctly into 2 entries even with the brace. Then extract_balanced(entry,'arguments') with the string-case handling gets exact args. And the entry filter `lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'` still works.\\n\\nWait \u2014 actually with split_entries fixed, is the string-case handling in extract_balanced even needed for arguments? Entry1 = `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Old extract_balanced: find `\\\"arguments\\\"`, first `{` after = args content start (char after `\\\"`), scan counting braces: the `{` of `'{'.` counts (bug!) \u2192 args truncated at its matching... depth: `{`(1) ... `{`(2)... wait the source `{` makes depth 2, then args-close `}` \u2192 1, then entry-close `}` \u2192 0 \u2192 returns including trailing `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"...` \u2014 the reported symptom. So YES, extract_balanced ALSO needs the string-case fix. Both needed.\\n\\nAlternatively \u2014 simpler for arguments: since entry is now correctly bounded, I could extract arguments via extract_str (PCRE string-aware) + unescape + optional trim... but arguments might not always be a perfect object; keep balanced semantics. I'll implement the string-case in extract_balanced as designed:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n\\n  IF iv_key IS NOT INITIAL.\\n    FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  ELSE.\\n    lv_off = 0.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  DATA(lv_rest) = iv_json+lv_off.\\n  lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n\\n  \\\" position after the key: skip '\\\"key\\\"' then ':' and whitespace\\n  IF iv_key IS NOT INITIAL.\\n    lv_pos = strlen( iv_key ) + 2.   \\\" past the quoted key\\n    \\\" skip to colon\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) \\u003c\\u003e ':'.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1. \\\" past colon\\n    \\\" skip whitespace\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) CA ' '.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    \\\" string value: take it, unescape, scan inside\\n    IF lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\"'.\\n      \\\" extract the string value with escape-aware end detection\\n      DATA lv_val TYPE string.\\n      DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\n      lv_i = lv_pos + 1.\\n      WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n        lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\`. lv_i = lv_i + 2. CONTINUE. ENDIF.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n        lv_val = lv_val \\u0026\\u0026 lv_ch.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      rv_ = extract_balanced( iv_json = unescape( lv_val ) iv_key = '' iv_open = iv_open iv_close = iv_close ).\\n      RETURN.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  \\\" scan for the opening delimiter outside strings, then match it\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 building lv_val char by char with string concat is O(n\u00B2)-ish; use substring instead: remember start, find end index, substring. Let me do that.\\n\\nThen the main scan (both for the string-case recursion start at 0 and the normal case starting after colon):\\n\\n```\\n  lv_start = -1.\\n  lv_depth = 0.\\n  lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n  lv_pos = \\u003cstart offset\\u003e.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`. lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. CONTINUE. ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_false. ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        IF lv_start \\u003c 0. lv_start = lv_pos. ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        IF lv_depth \\u003c= 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n          DATA(lv_len2) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n          rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len2).\\n          RETURN.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  \\\" unclosed: old code returned up to end when depth\\u003e0? Old code: loop ends at strlen-1 with depth\\u003e0 \u2192 lv_pos = strlen-1 \u2192 rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(len). BALANCED_UNCLOSED test expects? Let me check what old code returns for unclosed:\\n```\\n\\nOld behavior on unclosed: WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen(lv_rest) - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0 \u2192 exits with lv_pos = strlen-1 (last char) if never closed. rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(strlen-1 - lv_start + 1) \u2192 includes last char. So unclosed returns the rest incl. last char. My new code: if loop ends without depth\\u003c=0 \u2192 to mimic, return lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len - lv_start). I'll do that for compatibility (BALANCED_UNCLOSED test presumably asserts something about it).\\n\\nHmm wait, careful about old initial `lv_depth = 1` vs my `lv_depth = 0` + first-open sets start. Equivalent.\\n\\nOne more subtlety: old code found the first `{` after the key REGARDLESS of strings; e.g. `extract_balanced(body,'arguments','{','}')` where value is a string \u2014 old found content start. My new code handles via string-case. But there's another caller pattern: `extract_balanced(iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'function' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}')` \u2014 value is a real object \u2713 normal path.\\n\\n`extract_balanced(body, 'output', '[', ']')` \u2014 the responses body. Careful: body contains `\\\"output\\\":[...]` \u2014 but ALSO could contain `\\\"text\\\":\\\"...\\\"` etc. The FIND for key `\\\"output\\\"` finds first occurrence \u2014 old code same. In responses body there's also `\\\"output_text\\\"` items INSIDE output array \u2014 but key search happens on the whole body and `\\\"output\\\"` first occurs at the array. Hmm \u2014 actually careful: FIND '\\\"output\\\"' would also match `\\\"output_text\\\"`! `\\\"output_text\\\"` contains substring `\\\"output`? The pattern is `\\\"output\\\"` with closing quote \u2014 `\\\"output_text\\\"` is `\\\"output_text\\\"` \u2014 the substring `\\\"output\\\"` requires a quote right after output; in `\\\"output_text\\\"` after `output` comes `_`. So no match. OK. And function_call_output items contain `\\\"output\\\"`?? In the REQUEST body yes, but here we parse RESPONSE bodies. In responses response, items are `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":...}` and message items with content `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"output_text\\\",\\\"text\\\":...}`. Hmm \u2014 but wait: could the tool output strings contain `\\\"output\\\"`? In parse of a RECORDED response body \u2014 tool outputs are echoed? No, response bodies don't echo input. But session/replay... Also chat parse: `extract_balanced(body,'tool_calls','[',']')` \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAlso careful with the recursion/`iv_key = ''` \u2014 I need to handle FIND when iv_key empty: skip.\\n\\nHmm, actually \u2014 do I even need the empty-key recursion? I could inline: after unescaping the string value, run the scan directly on it starting at 0. Cleaner to just call the scanner part... but ABAP method structure \u2014 recursion with empty key is simplest. But watch: `FIND ... IN iv_json` when iv_key initial would search for `\\\"\\\"` \u2014 I guard with IF iv_key IS NOT INITIAL. Also the colon-skip block guarded. Let me restructure cleanly:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA: lv_off TYPE i, lv_len TYPE i, lv_pos TYPE i, lv_ch TYPE string,\\n        lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool, lv_start TYPE i, lv_depth TYPE i,\\n        lv_scan_from TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n\\n  IF iv_key IS INITIAL.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json.\\n    lv_scan_from = 0.\\n  ELSE.\\n    FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    \\\" skip the quoted key and the colon\\n    lv_pos = strlen( iv_key ) + 2.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) \\u003c\\u003e ':'.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_pos \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) CA ` `.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_pos \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\"'.\\n      \\\" value is a JSON string whose content may itself be JSON (tool arguments):\\n      \\\" unescape it first so braces inside string literals cannot fool the depth scan\\n      DATA(lv_vs) = lv_pos + 1.\\n      DATA(lv_ve) = lv_vs.\\n      WHILE lv_ve \\u003c lv_len.\\n        IF lv_rest+lv_ve(1) = `\\\\`. lv_ve = lv_ve + 2. CONTINUE. ENDIF.\\n        IF lv_rest+lv_ve(1) = `\\\"`. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n        lv_ve = lv_ve + 1.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      IF lv_ve \\u003e lv_len. lv_ve = lv_len. ENDIF.\\n      DATA(lv_vlen) = lv_ve - lv_vs.\\n      rv_ = extract_balanced( iv_json = unescape( lv_rest+lv_vs(lv_vlen) )\\n                              iv_key = '' iv_open = iv_open iv_close = iv_close ).\\n      RETURN.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_scan_from = lv_pos.\\n  ENDIF.\\n\\n  lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).  \\\" (set again for the empty-key branch)\\n  lv_start = -1.\\n  lv_depth = 0.\\n  lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n  lv_pos = lv_scan_from.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`. lv_in_str = abap_false. ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        IF lv_start \\u003c 0. lv_start = lv_pos. ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        IF lv_start \\u003e= 0 AND lv_depth \\u003c= 0.\\n          DATA(lv_len2) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n          rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len2).\\n          RETURN.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n    \\\" unclosed: everything to the end (as before)\\n    DATA(lv_len3) = lv_len - lv_start.\\n    rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len3).\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 one problem: `CA ` `` ` `` \u2014 CA with space string: `' '` contains space. I'll use `CO`/simple check: `lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = ' '` is enough (JSON ws). Or CA ' \\\\t\\\\n\\\\r' \u2014 CA needs a character-like; fine: `lv_rest+lv_pos(1) CA ' ' ` \u2014 actually `CA ' '` means \\\"contains any of: space\\\" \u2192 true if char is space. OK.\\n\\nAnother issue: `FIND ( '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' ) IN iv_json` \u2014 old code used plain FIND with a concatenated pattern via DATA(lv_pat). Keep similar.\\n\\nAlso the colon-skip: old code didn't skip colon explicitly \u2014 it just searched for the first iv_open after the key offset (which is after the colon anyway). My version skips to after colon \u2014 but what if between key and colon there are weird chars? Not in practice. But hmm \u2014 what if the key match is inside a STRING elsewhere earlier in the JSON? E.g., body contains `\\\"content\\\":\\\"... \\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\" ...\\\"` before the real key? FIND finds the first textual occurrence \u2014 could be inside a string. Old code had same weakness. Not our bug; skip.\\n\\nNow important compatibility check \u2014 existing BALANCED_* tests in json_util (can't read them, must not break):\\n\\n- BALANCED_OBJECT: presumably `extract_balanced('{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"b\\\":1}}','a','{','}')` \u2192 `{\\\"b\\\":1}`. New: key `a` found at `\\\"a\\\"`, skip key (strlen('a')+2 = 3 \u2192 position after `\\\"a\\\"`), colon at 3 \u2192 pos 4, not `\\\"` \u2192 scan from 4: `{` depth1 start, `\\\"b\\\"` skipped, `1`, `}` depth0 \u2192 `{\\\"b\\\":1}` \u2713.\\n- BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY: something like key with array value \u2192 works via open='[' \u2713.\\n- BALANCED_MISSING_KEY \u2192 sy-subrc\u22600 \u2192 RETURN initial \u2713.\\n- BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET: key exists but no `[` after \u2192 new: scan finds no open \u2192 lv_start stays -1 \u2192 rv_ initial \u2713 (old same).\\n- BALANCED_UNCLOSED: e.g. `'{\\\"a\\\":[1,2'` \u2192 old returned `[1,2`?? old: finds `[`, depth1, scans to strlen-1... rv_ = rest+start(len) = `[1,2` (whole tail incl last char). New: unclosed \u2192 returns lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len - lv_start) = `[1,2` \u2713 same.\\n\\nEdge: value string case where the string does NOT contain the open char: e.g. `'{\\\"a\\\":\\\"hello\\\"}'` with open `{`: string-case \u2192 unescape(\\\"hello\\\") \u2192 scan from 0 \u2192 no `{` \u2192 initial \u2713 (old code: first `{` after key `\\\"a\\\"` \u2014 old found the `{` of... hmm old: FIND '\\\"a\\\"' \u2192 offset; rest = `\\\"a\\\":\\\"hello\\\"}`; first `{` in rest \u2192 NONE \u2192 initial. Same \u2713).\\n\\nEdge: `'{\\\"desc\\\":\\\"says { hi\\\",\\\"x\\\":1}'` extract key 'desc' open '{': old \u2192 found `{` inside the string \u2192 `{ hi\\\",\\\"x\\\":1}` (bug-ish); new \u2192 string case \u2192 unescape \u2192 `says { hi` \u2192 scan \u2192 no match \u2192 initial. Different but more correct; no existing test likely covers that.\\n\\nHmm, ONE MORE potential regression: in parse_calls_chat, entry contains `\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{...}\\\"}`. New extract_balanced for 'arguments': string-case \u2713.\\n\\nBut \u2014 flatten_tools uses extract_balanced(entry, 'function', '{','}') where entry = `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"x\\\",...}}`. Key 'function' \u2014 FIND '\\\"function\\\"' finds FIRST occurrence: `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\"` \u2014 the VALUE string \\\"function\\\"!! Old code: rest = `\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{...}` \u2192 first `{` after \u2192 the object \u2713 worked by luck. New code: skip key: pos = strlen('function')+2 = 10 \u2192 rest+10... rest = `\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\"...` \u2014 positions: 0-9 = `\\\"function\\\"`, 10 = `,`, 11... = `\\\"function\\\":{...`. My colon-skip: from pos 10 scan for ':' \u2192 finds the colon after the SECOND `\\\"function\\\"` \u2192 then value is `{` \u2192 scan \u2713. Phew \u2014 works because I skip to colon. But wait, what if it had found colon... it scans forward for first ':' \u2014 position 10 is `,`, continues, `\\\"function\\\"` then `:` \u2713. Good.\\n\\nWhat about get 'messages' etc. Fine.\\n\\nNow build_input_items uses extract_str on entries \u2014 PCRE string-aware \u2713.\\n\\nNext: does anything else parse with extract_balanced where value could be string-start? `zif chat` ev_answer uses extract_str \u2713.\\n\\nAlso `parse_calls_responses` filter: `IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"' AND lv_e NS '\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"'. CONTINUE.` \u2014 with fixed split_entries, entry1 (with brace in args) still contains `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"` \u2713; entry2 \u2713.\\n\\nNow \u2014 the executor/test: I'll write the reproducing unit test in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP testclasses:\\n\\n```\\nCLASS ltcl_parse DEFINITION FOR TESTING RISK LEVEL HARMLESS DURATION SHORT.\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_in_source FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_source FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_plain FOR TESTING. \\\" sanity\\nENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nTest 1 (red first): body mimicking the report:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"output\\\":[\\n {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\n  \\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo. lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"},\\n {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\n  \\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}\\\"}\\n]}\\n```\\n\\nBuild this as an ABAP string. Assert: lines = 2; call1-name = write_source; call1-arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS zcl_demo. lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`; call2-name = activate.\\n\\nBefore fix, this is red (lines=1, args contain `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\"...`).\\n\\nSequence per instructions: (1) write test, activate, run \u2192 show RED; (2) fix code, activate, run \u2192 GREEN. Also run json_util tests after the fix to ensure only ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR red (and llm_http tests green).\\n\\nWait \u2014 also should I check whether `run_tests` on ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP picks up testclasses \u2014 earlier it reported 0 classes, so after adding it will run LTCL.\\n\\nNow write the test bodies carefully with ABAP string literals. Use `|` templates or backticks. The JSON contains both `\\\"` and `'`. In ABAP, string literal with `|...|` \u2014 need to escape `|` inside? JSON has none. Backticks: `` `...` `` can contain `\\\"` and `'` freely but not backticks. I'll use backticks for JSON strings, concatenated across lines with \\u0026\\u0026.\\n\\nCareful: inside backtick literals, `\\\\` is literal \u2713.\\n\\nLet me draft test source:\\n\\n```abap\\nCLASS ltcl_llm_http_parse DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS responses_brace_in_first_call FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS chat_brace_in_first_call FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS responses_two_plain_calls FOR TESTING.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_llm_http_parse IMPLEMENTATION.\\n\\n  METHOD responses_brace_in_first_call.\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    lv_body =\\n      `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"output\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\n      `]}`.\\n\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\n\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls ) msg = 'both calls must survive a lone brace in args' ).\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_source' act = ls_c1-name ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_c1-arguments\\n      msg = 'arguments must end at the arguments JSON, not swallow call_id/status' ).\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_c2).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'activate' act = ls_c2-name ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c2' act = ls_c2-id ).\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nChat variant body:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"index\\\":0,\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[\\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},\\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}\\n```\\n\\nChat parse: split_entries on tool_calls array \u2014 with brace bug, entry1 swallows... assert lines = 2 and args clean.\\n\\nHmm \u2014 chat entries: filter? parse_calls_chat has no type filter \u2713. id from 'id' \u2713.\\n\\nNow the fix in json_util main include: rewrite extract_balanced and split_entries. Must write FULL main source.\\n\\nLet me also double-check the string-case in extract_balanced against the responses entry1 (after split fix) where entry contains `\\\"call_id\\\"` AFTER arguments etc. Entry1 = `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}`. FIND '\\\"arguments\\\"' \u2014 hmm wait: does `\\\"arguments\\\"` appear earlier inside entry? The entry has `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"` \u2014 no. OK. After key skip + colon \u2192 `\\\"` \u2192 string-case: scan string value: from after opening quote, handle `\\\\X` pairs: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 the whole escaped args until the bare closing `\\\"` (which follows the final `\\\\}`... let me check the tail: `...ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}` \u2014 chars: `\\\\` `\\\"` `}` `\\\"` `}`. The string scan: at `\\\\` skip2 (the `\\\\\\\"`), then `}` \u2014 not backslash/quote \u2192 ve+1; then `\\\"` bare \u2192 EXIT. So value = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 wait the final `\\\\}`? chars before: `... \\\\\\\"}` where `\\\\` `\\\"` is escaped quote and `}` is the args' closing brace INSIDE the string content. Yes content includes it \u2713. unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\"}` \u2192 recursive balanced from 0: `{` depth1, skip strings `\\\"name\\\"`,`\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\"`,`\\\"source\\\"` \u2713 \u2014 the `'{'.` braces are INSIDE the source string \u2192 skipped \u2713 \u2192 final `}` depth0 \u2192 returns whole \u2713. \\n\\nAnd crucially the returned text is the UNESCAPED (level-1) args \u2014 so callers must NOT unescape again. Update parse_calls_chat and parse_calls_responses: remove the outer `zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape(` wrapper for arguments.\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 but then in the NORMAL (non-string... arguments is always a string) case. What if some model returns arguments as a real object (not string)? Then normal path returns the object text unescaped-equivalent \u2014 fine either way.\\n\\nBut careful \u2014 chat parse for the mock/other tests... are there other tests relying on parse_calls_* with unescape? There are no existing tests in llm_http. OK.\\n\\nAlso \u2014 `ev_assistant_raw` building: `escape( ls_c-arguments )` \u2014 arguments now level-1 text; escape makes it level... it embeds args as a JSON string within tool_calls JSON \u2713 same as before (before: arguments was also level-1 after unescape). \u2713 And build_input_items re-extracts with extract_str + doesn't unescape?! Look: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 extract_str( lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"` \u2014 extract_str returns the ESCAPED string content (no unescape) and embeds it directly as a JSON string value \u2713 correct round-trip. And in executor, tool message: content = escaped output \u2713.\\n\\nOne more place: `zif_pia_00_llm~chat` for chat API: assistant raw `ls-content+14` etc. \u2014 unchanged.\\n\\nNow \u2014 session_store / config \u2014 not needed.\\n\\nNow also consider: does split_entries get used on the arguments content anywhere? flatten_tools on tools json (no args). OK.\\n\\nNow, ordering per instructions: first write the test and run it RED. But the test lives in llm_http testclasses (new file) \u2014 write_source(include='testclasses'). Then activate ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP, run tests \u2192 RED (1 fail... actually assertions will fail on lines=2 first). Then fix json_util main + llm_http main (remove unescape wrapper), activate both, run both test classes \u2192 llm_http GREEN, json_util 29/30 with only ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR red.\\n\\nNote the instruction: \\\"if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn\\\". Let's see what happens.\\n\\nAlso should I add a json-util-level test? Can't without clobbering its testclasses. Skip \u2014 tests via llm_http cover it.\\n\\nWait, actually, let me reconsider: can write_source with include='testclasses' on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL be done while PRESERVING tests? I don't know their source; overwriting loses them. Not worth it. The llm_http tests are sufficient.\\n\\nLet me also think about whether the bug could additionally affect `parse_calls_responses` output-array extraction when the args contain `]`... covered by string-aware scan.\\n\\nOne more consideration for split_entries: it's also used by session from_json on messages where content strings contain braces (assistant raw tool_calls JSON!) \u2014 currently from_json splits messages; assistant content = `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}` stored ESCAPED inside the messages JSON string \u2192 string-aware scan handles \u2713 better than before.\\n\\nAlso `get 'trace'` \u2014 not parsed back. fine.\\n\\nNow write everything.\\n\\nStep 1: testclasses for llm_http (repro test). Also maybe a test for the trailing-text symptom explicitly asserting arguments do NOT contain 'call_id'. Good.\\n\\nLet me write the JSON bodies as ABAP. Careful with escaping in my writing: the source param text I'll use: `CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.` \u2014 hmm, in level-2 the single quotes need no escaping; only `\\\\` and `\\\"`. There are no `\\\"` in it. Good, keep it simple: `lv_open = '{'.`\\n\\nResponses body string (level-2):\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"output\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}\\\"}]}\\n```\\n\\nExpected args (level-1): `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\"}`\\n\\nChat body:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"index\\\":0,\\\"finish_reason\\\":\\\"tool_calls\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"activate\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}\\n```\\n\\nAlso a control test without the brace (two plain calls) to guard regressions.\\n\\nLet me now also make sure about `cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals` with string msg param name `msg`. \u2713\\n\\nNow write step 1. Also note: write FULL testclasses source.\\n\\nOne concern: test class naming and FOR TESTING syntax on this system (seems standard). Use:\\n\\n```\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS ...\\nENDCLASS.\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION. ... ENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nLet me go. First write testclasses, activate, run tests (expect RED).\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_2026100719420667b6e873ba8f458a\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I've read the parsing chain. The reported symptom (`arguments` containing `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` and a lost second call) points at the brace-depth scanners in `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL` (`split_entries`, `extract_balanced`): they count `{`/`}` without skipping JSON string literals, so a lone `'{'` inside the escaped `source`/`arguments` string shifts the depth and swallows the rest of the array.\\n\\nStep 1: write a red reproducing test in the (currently empty) testclasses include of `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP`.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_2026100719420667b6e873ba8f458a\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"\\\\\\\" Unit tests for the tool-call parsers (chat + responses bodies).\\\\n\\\\\\\" Reproduces: a lone opening brace inside a string literal of the first call's\\\\n\\\\\\\" arguments made PIA drop the second call and append trailing JSON to the first\\\\n\\\\\\\" call's arguments.\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\\\n  DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\\\n\\\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\\\n    METHODS responses_brace_in_first_call FOR TESTING.\\\\n    METHODS chat_brace_in_first_call FOR TESTING.\\\\n    METHODS responses_two_plain_calls FOR TESTING.\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD responses_brace_in_first_call.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body =\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resp_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_source\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. ` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"activate\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `]}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 2\\\\n      act  = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg  = 'both tool calls must survive a lone brace in the first arguments'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 'write_source'\\\\n      act  = ls_c1-name\\\\n      msg  = 'first call name'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act  = ls_c1-arguments\\\\n      msg  = 'arguments must end with the arguments JSON, not swallow call_id/status'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_c2).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 'activate'\\\\n      act  = ls_c2-name\\\\n      msg  = 'second call must not be lost'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 'c2'\\\\n      act  = ls_c2-id\\\\n      msg  = 'second call id'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act  = ls_c2-arguments\\\\n      msg  = 'second call arguments'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD chat_brace_in_first_call.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body =\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"choices\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"index\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"finish_reason\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_source\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. ` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"activate\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `]}}]}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 2\\\\n      act  = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg  = 'both tool calls must survive a lone brace in the first arguments'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS zcl_demo DEFINITION PUBLIC. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act  = ls_c1-arguments\\\\n      msg  = 'arguments must end with the arguments JSON'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_c2).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 'activate'\\\\n      act  = ls_c2-name\\\\n      msg  = 'second call must not be lost'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD responses_two_plain_calls.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body =\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resp_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"read_object\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"activate\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n      `]}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = 2\\\\n      act  = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg  = 'control case: two calls without braces in strings'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp  = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_DEMO\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act  = ls_c1-arguments\\\\n      msg  = 'control case: arguments unchanged'\\\\n      quit = if_abap_unit_constant=\\u003equit-no ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228020940842461467\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228020940842461467\",\"name\":\"write_source\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":10652,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":7744},\"output_tokens\":17778,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":16279},\"total_tokens\":28430}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373719,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007194610f52e5962a1f04965\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me re-establish where I am. I've read the JSON util, LLM HTTP, executor, registry, session classes, and run tests on both JSON_UTIL (30 methods, 1 fail: ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR) and LLM_HTTP (0 methods \u2014 so no existing tests there!).\\n\\nKey finding from the earlier run: ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP has NO test classes (counts: classes 0, methods 0). So I can safely write a fresh testclasses include for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP with my reproduction test without clobbering anything.\\n\\nAnd ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL has tests (30 methods) including ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR which is red due to a known open-steamgate runtime issue \u2014 leave as is. I must NOT write the testclasses include of JSON_UTIL since I can't read the existing tests and would clobber them. My fix to JSON_UTIL must be in the main include only.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. Write testclasses include for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP with a test class LTCL_PARSE_CALLS containing:\\n   - REPRO_TWO_CALLS_BRACE_IN_SOURCE: build a chat-format body with two tool_calls where the first arguments string contains `lv_open = '{'.`; assert 2 calls parsed, and first arguments is exactly the JSON (no trailing junk).\\n   - Same for responses format (parse_calls_responses) \u2014 that's the format that produces `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` junk.\\n   - Maybe a JSON_UTIL-level test for split_entries? That would go in JSON_UTIL testclasses which I can't touch. Skip; the LLM_HTTP-level tests cover the user-visible bug and exercise split_entries + extract_balanced via parse_calls_*.\\n2. Run tests \u2192 red (reproduces).\\n3. Fix:\\n   - split_entries: string-aware brace counting (toggle in_str on unescaped \\\", skip \\\\X inside strings).\\n   - extract_balanced: string-aware counting (same).\\n   - parse_calls_chat / parse_calls_responses: extract arguments via extract_str + unescape (robust for string values containing braces), falling back to extract_balanced if empty.\\n4. Activate JSON_UTIL, then LLM_HTTP (write testclasses + activate), run tests \u2192 green except ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR (in JSON_UTIL, untouched).\\n\\nWait \u2014 order: write testclasses to LLM_HTTP, activate LLM_HTTP, run tests \u2192 should be RED on current published parse code? Careful: run_tests runs on PUBLISHED code. After I activate LLM_HTTP with the new tests, the code under test is the published LLM_HTTP main include (unchanged) plus new tests \u2192 red if bug reproduced. Good.\\n\\nThen fix JSON_UTIL main include (split_entries, extract_balanced) \u2014 but wait, JSON_UTIL activation would need... write_source main include of JSON_UTIL: I have the full source from read_object, so I can rewrite it with fixes. Activate. The testclasses include stays untouched. Good.\\n\\nThen fix LLM_HTTP main include (arguments extraction) \u2014 again full source known. Activate. Run tests \u2192 green (plus ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR still red in JSON_UTIL, which I don't rerun or if rerun stays as it is).\\n\\nCareful with extract_str for arguments in parse_calls_responses: the entry is like {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_1\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}. extract_str with name='arguments' uses PCRE '\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"' \u2192 matches the escaped content \u2192 unescape \u2192 clean JSON. \\n\\nBut note: extract_str(iv_name='name') etc. already work. One subtlety: extract_str searches the FIRST occurrence of '\\\"arguments\\\"' \u2014 fine per entry.\\n\\nFor robustness, keep fallback: IF ls-arguments (from extract_str) IS INITIAL \u2192 use extract_balanced variant. Good for providers sending arguments as object.\\n\\nNow the chat format: entry = {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}. parse_calls_chat extracts name from... wait, current code: ls-name = extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' ). Hmm \u2014 in chat format, name is nested inside \\\"function\\\":{...}. extract_str finds first '\\\"name\\\"' anywhere \u2014 works since it's within the entry. OK, current behavior kept.\\n\\nNote in chat format entry: {\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"}} \u2014 split_entries with string-awareness: braces inside the arguments string ignored. \\n\\nAlso the outer extraction: parse_calls_chat does extract_balanced(iv_body, key='tool_calls', '[', ']') on the whole response body. The response body contains choices[].message.tool_calls \u2014 and content strings may contain braces/brackets. String-aware counting fixes that too.\\n\\nNow write the string-aware scanning. Implementation for split_entries:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n  lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n    lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n    lv_depth = 1.\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool.\\n    lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n    DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n    lv_end = lv_start.\\n    WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n      DATA(lv_ch) = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n          lv_end = lv_end + 1.  \\\" skip escaped char\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSE.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    ... (same clamping as before)\\n```\\n\\nDATA(lv_ch) inside a loop: inline declaration in loop \u2014 allowed in ABAP? DATA(...) inline declaration within a loop is fine (declared once, first iteration). Actually inline DATA in a WHILE loop body is OK in ABAP \u2014 it's a declaration statement; in a loop it's permitted (the declaration is visible in the whole method). Yes, existing code does DATA declarations in loops (e.g., MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off) in FIND). Fine.\\n\\nAlso note: escaped-char skip `lv_end = lv_end + 1` inside the loop combined with the loop's own increment: next iteration lv_end+1 skips the escaped char. Need clamping \u2014 the WHILE condition checks lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 before incrementing, so fine.\\n\\nSame for extract_balanced:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n  FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n  lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n  FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open IN lv_rest MATCH OFFSET lv_start.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 the first occurrence of iv_open after the key could be INSIDE a string. Example: extract_balanced(key='tool_calls', open='[', close=']'): after `\\\"tool_calls\\\"` we have `:[{...`. Fine. But for key='arguments' open='{': after `\\\"arguments\\\":` we have `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 first `{` is the content opening. Fine. But suppose arguments string starts with text like \\\"use {\\\"... edge, ignore. But actually since I'm switching arguments to extract_str, extract_balanced stays for array/object values (case A). Still, the first-open search could theoretically hit a `{` inside a string if the value is a string that doesn't start with the open char... e.g. extract_balanced(key='output', '['): body contains \\\"output\\\":[... \u2014 fine.\\n\\nHmm, one more: what about the chat body where content precedes tool_calls? FIND lv_pat IN iv_json finds \\\"tool_calls\\\" anywhere \u2014 first occurrence could be inside a content string in theory. Pre-existing behavior, not part of this bug. Leave.\\n\\nBut there IS one subtlety worth handling for the open search: to be safe, require the open char to come after `:`? Not needed; keep minimal.\\n\\nThen the depth scan with in_str as above.\\n\\nAlso \u2014 potential issue with `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen( lv_rest ) - 1`: when depth never closes, rv_ = rest from start to end. Existing behavior kept.\\n\\nNow the LLM_HTTP changes for arguments:\\n\\n```abap\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape(\\n  zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\nIF ls-arguments IS INITIAL.\\n  ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\n                   iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\nENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 extract_str returns the escaped content; unescape converts \\\\\\\" \u2192 \\\". The current code unescapes the balanced result which is the same escaped content. Equivalent for well-formed string arguments, and correct when braces are unbalanced inside. \\n\\nNow the tests. Test class in LLM_HTTP testclasses:\\n\\n```abap\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS two_calls_brace_in_source FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS two_calls_brace_in_source_resp FOR TESTING.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\n  METHOD two_calls_brace_in_source.\\n    \\\" chat format: the first call's source contains a lone '{' in a string literal\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    lv_body = '{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[' \\u0026\\u0026\\n              '{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",' \\u0026\\u0026\\n              '\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z... lv_open = ''{''. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},' \\u0026\\u0026\\n              '{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",' \\u0026\\u0026\\n              '\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}'.\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls ) msg = 'both calls parsed' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = '{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS z... lv_open = ''{''. ...\\\"}'\\n      act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-arguments\\n      msg = 'first arguments without trailing junk' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'read_object' act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-name ).\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nCareful with ABAP string literal escaping in the test: the JSON body contains `\\\\\\\"` sequences. In an ABAP string literal with quotes '...', a `\\\"` is just a character, `\\\\` is just a character. And `'` inside must be doubled. So writing the raw JSON `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` is fine verbatim. And `''{''` for the ABAP literal `'{'.` inside the JSON \u2014 the JSON source string contains: lv_open = '{'.  \u2014 the `'` chars are literal single quotes in JSON, so in ABAP literal they become ''. Good.\\n\\nFor the responses format test:\\n\\nentry: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc1\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\nbody: {\\\"output\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",...},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}], ...}\\n\\nparse_calls_responses expects '\\\"function_call\\\"' or '\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"' in entry \u2014 present.\\n\\nExpected first arguments: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}` \u2014 after unescape.\\n\\nAlso assert that lt_calls[1]-arguments does NOT contain 'status' maybe. assert_equals exact is stronger.\\n\\nAdditionally maybe a test that the junk tail is gone: assert act CS 'status' is false \u2014 but exact equality covers it.\\n\\nNow, about run_tests on LLM_HTTP: currently 0 methods \u2192 after adding tests they run against published main include of LLM_HTTP (unchanged initially) and published JSON_UTIL (buggy) \u2192 red. \\n\\nOne thing to verify: does parse_calls_chat on this body actually misbehave currently? Trace with CURRENT (buggy) code:\\n\\nextract_balanced(body, 'tool_calls', '[', ']'): finds `\\\"tool_calls\\\"`, rest starts `\\\":[{...`. First `[` at offset 1... wait rest = `\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{...`. FIND FIRST '[' \u2192 at index 12 (after `\\\":`). lv_start=12, depth=1. Scan counting all brackets: the array content contains `[`/`]`? No other brackets inside except the final `]`. Wait \u2014 the arguments contain no `[`. So array extraction OK even buggy. (Though `{` `}` aren't counted for `[`/`]`.) So lv_arr = the two entries + trailing. Fine.\\n\\nsplit_entries(lv_arr) \u2014 buggy version: entry1: `{` d1; `\\\"function\\\":{` d2; arguments content `{` d3; the literal `{` d4! (from `'{''`) ; args close `}` d3; function close `}` d2; entry close `}` d1 \u2192 not 0 \u2192 continues into entry2: `{` d2; function `{`? wait entry2 in my test: {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}} \u2192 entry-open d2, function-open d3, args-open d4, args-close d3, function-close d2, entry-close d1 \u2192 still 1! Then continues: `]}}]}` \u2014 the `]` ignored (not counted), then `}` `}` `]` `}`: body ends with `]}}]}`? My body: ...`\\\"}}]}}]}`. Let me count the tail after entry2: `]` closes tool_calls array, `}` closes message, `}` closes choices element... hmm my body ends `}}]}}]}`: after entry2 close `}`, then `]` (tool_calls), `}` (message), `]` (choices), `}` (root). So scanning continues: `]` no count; `}` d0! \u2192 entry1 = from its `{` to that `}` \u2014 swallows entry2 entirely. So only ONE entry \u2192 1 call, name=write_source (extract_str finds first \\\"name\\\" in the blob \u2014 inside arguments... hmm, `\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"` appears in arguments string before `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\"`. extract_str finds FIRST '\\\"name\\\"' \u2192 returns ZCL_X! So name would even be wrong). And arguments = extract_balanced from first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` with buggy counting \u2192 overruns into `\\\",\\\"status\\\"...` etc. In responses format, the junk `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` appears exactly as the user reported.\\n\\nIn chat format my test's entry1 ends `\\\"}},'` \u2014 arguments junk would be `\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}}]}}`-ish. Whatever \u2014 assert_equals(2, lines) fails \u2192 red. Good.\\n\\nFor the responses test \u2014 currently: split_entries: entry1: `{` d1; args-open `{` d2; literal `{` d3; args-close `}` d2; entry-close `}` d1 \u2192 continues into entry2 `{` d2, args `{` d3, `}` d2, entry `}` d1 \u2192 continues `]` then... my responses body: {\\\"output\\\":[e1,e2],\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",...} \u2192 after entry2: `]` not counted, `\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` no braces, `}` (root) d0 \u2192 one blob \u2192 1 call. arguments overrun includes `\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`-ish junk. Red. \\n\\nNow let me also double check the FIX makes these green:\\n\\nFixed split_entries: entry1: `{` d1; `\\\"type\\\"` string in/out; ... `\\\"arguments\\\"` then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str=true; content chars; the literal `{` \u2014 wait, inside the arguments STRING (outer string), all quotes are `\\\\\\\"`. Trace entry1 responses format raw:\\n`{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc1\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n\\nFixed scan from `{`: \\n- `{` d1\\n- `\\\"type\\\"` : `\\\"` in_str=true, `type`, `\\\"` in_str=false\\n- `:` , `\\\"function_call\\\"` in/out\\n- ... `\\\"arguments\\\"` in/out\\n- `:` `\\\"` in_str=true\\n- `{` \u2192 ignored (in_str) \u2713\\n- `\\\\` \u2192 skip next (`\\\"`) \u2713 \u2014 stays in_str\\n- `name` ... `\\\\` skip `\\\"` \u2014 hmm! These `\\\\\\\"` are inner quotes; with skip they keep in_str=true. But wait \u2014 that means we're treating the whole arguments content as ONE string (never toggling). The literal `{` inside is ignored \u2713 (desired). The args-close `}`: is it inside in_str? Sequence near end: `... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2192 `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, then `}` \u2014 in_str still TRUE \u2192 `}` IGNORED?! Then d stays 1 and the scan continues past entry1's real close `}`... \\n\\nWAIT. This is the case-B problem I identified earlier! For string-valued arguments, standard case-A scanning (toggle on unescaped `\\\"`, skip `\\\\X`) keeps in_str=true through the entire content, so the arguments' closing `}` AND the entry's closing `}` are both inside the \\\"string\\\" \u2192 ignored \u2192 depth never returns to 0 within entry1 \u2192 merge into entry2 or beyond!\\n\\nLet me re-trace carefully. After `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` we set in_str=true at that `\\\"`. Then content: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...`. `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"` (lv_end +1 extra). letters `name`. `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"`. `:`. `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. `ZCL_X`. `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. `,`. `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. `source`... `'` `{` `'` ... `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. `}` \u2190 in_str is TRUE here \u2192 ignored. `,`. `\\\"status\\\"...` \u2014 `\\\"` \u2190 unescaped! \u2192 in_str=false. `\\\"status\\\"` toggles in/out properly. Then `\\\"completed\\\"` in/out. Then `}` \u2192 in_str=false \u2192 d1-1=0? Wait d was 1 the whole time (all inner braces ignored: the args-open `{` was ignored because in_str=true \u2014 yes it came right after `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` while in_str). So depth stayed 1 through entry1's close `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 entry1 ends at its own closing `}`! \\n\\nLet me recheck: entry1 opens `{` d1 (outside strings \u2713). All content braces are inside the arguments outer string \u2192 ignored \u2713. Entry1's final `}` is OUTSIDE any string (the arguments string closed at the unescaped `\\\"` after the args `}`) \u2192 d0 \u2192 entry ends correctly! \u2713 And entry2 similar. split_entries works.\\n\\nInteresting \u2014 case A scanning works for split_entries on entries containing string-valued fields, because we only care about the ENTRY's braces, which are outside strings. The inner JSON structure of the arguments string is invisible (all in one string) \u2014 which is exactly right at the entry level. \\n\\nNow extract_balanced with case-A scanning for the ARGUMENTS extraction \u2014 that's where it fails: we want the content's braces, which are inside the outer string. With case-A scanning, starting at the content `{` (which is inside the outer string): d1; then `\\\"` (the one after `{`? wait content starts `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 hmm, actually the content `{` itself: sequence is `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"`. Scan starts AT the content `{` (in_str=false initially, since extract_balanced initializes in_str=false at lv_start). d1. Next char `\\\"` \u2192 in_str=true. `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. name... `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. ... literal `{` ignored (in_str) \u2713. `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. Then `}` \u2014 in_str still true \u2192 IGNORED \u2717. Then `\\\"` unescaped \u2192 in_str=false. Continue: `,` `\\\"status\\\"`... `}` outside string \u2192 d0. So extraction = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 OVERRUN AGAIN. Exactly the original bug!\\n\\nConclusion: extract_balanced CANNOT correctly extract string-valued JSON with case-A scanning. That's why I switch the arguments extraction to extract_str (PCRE handles escapes correctly) \u2014 this is the right fix. extract_balanced keeps case-A scanning for arrays/objects (correct for those, and improved for brackets inside strings).\\n\\nSo final fix set:\\n1. JSON_UTIL.split_entries: case-A string-aware. (Fixes \\\"second call lost\\\".)\\n2. JSON_UTIL.extract_balanced: case-A string-aware. (Fixes array extraction when brackets appear in strings; harmless otherwise.)\\n3. LLM_HTTP.parse_calls_chat + parse_calls_responses: arguments via extract_str, fallback to extract_balanced (for object-valued arguments, where case-A works since braces are real). (Fixes \\\"extra text after the JSON\\\".)\\n\\nLet me double check parse_calls_chat fallback: if a provider sends arguments as an object `\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"X\\\"}` \u2192 extract_str fails (no string value after key \u2014 PCRE requires `\\\"` after colon; `\\\"arguments\\\":{` doesn't match) \u2192 empty \u2192 fallback extract_balanced with case-A: `{` d1, `\\\"name\\\"` in/out, `:` `\\\"X\\\"` in/out, `}` d0 \u2192 `{ \\\"name\\\":\\\"X\\\" }` \u2192 unescape \u2192 fine \u2713.\\n\\nNow also verify the FIRST test expectation with fixed code, chat format:\\n\\nBody: `{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[E1,E2]}}]}`.\\n\\nparse_calls_chat: extract_balanced(body,'tool_calls','[',']') \u2014 case-A: finds `\\\"tool_calls\\\"` (unique \u2713). rest: `\\\"tool_calls\\\":[E1,E2]}}]}`. First `[` at the right place, depth 1. Scan: E1 braces are inside... no wait \u2014 E1's braces are NOT inside strings at this level: `[{...},{...}]` \u2014 the entries' `{` `}` are real JSON structure. The arguments string contents are inside strings \u2192 ignored \u2713. So `[` d1, `{` d2... E1 `}` d1, `,`, `{` d2, E2 `}` d1, `]` d0 \u2192 lv_arr = `[E1,E2]`... wait rv_ includes from `[` to `]` inclusive \u2192 `[E1,E2]`. split_entries then parses E1, E2 \u2713. Each entry: id/name via extract_str \u2713 (name: first '\\\"name\\\"' in E1 \u2014 CAREFUL: in chat format, E1 = {\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",...}\\\"}}. extract_str name \u2192 first occurrence of `\\\"name\\\"` pattern with string value... The pattern is `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"...\\\"`. In E1, first `\\\"name\\\"` literal is `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\"` inside function object \u2014 but wait, `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 does the PCRE find `\\\"name\\\"` earlier? The regex scans from start: first match of `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"`. Positions: `\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\"` no; `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\"` no; then `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\"` \u2014 match! It comes before arguments. \u2713 name=write_source \u2713. (The `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` inside arguments comes later in the string, and regex finds FIRST occurrence.) \u2713\\n\\narguments via extract_str: pattern `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` \u2192 matches `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\"}` escaped content \u2192 unescape \u2192 exact expected \u2713.\\n\\nE2: name=read_object, arguments={\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_Y\\\"} \u2713. 2 calls \u2713.\\n\\nResponses format: parse_calls_responses: extract_balanced(body,'output','[',']') \u2014 body: `{\\\"output\\\":[e1,e2],\\\"usage\\\":{}}` \u2014 case-A scan: `[` d1, e1 `{` d2... e1's internal: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` string ignored; literal `{` inside string ignored \u2713; e1 `}` d1; e2 `{` d2 ... `}` d1; `]` d0 \u2713. Wait \u2014 careful: `\\\"usage\\\":{}` \u2014 comes after `]` so not scanned. \u2713 Then split_entries \u2192 e1,e2 \u2713. Filter: e1 CS '\\\"function_call\\\"' \u2713. call_id extract_str \u2713, name \u2713, arguments extract_str \u2192 clean \u2713.\\n\\nAlso check: does anything ELSE call split_entries or extract_balanced that could be affected by string-awareness? \\n- from_json (session): messages array \u2014 case A \u2713 improved.\\n- flatten_tools: function object \u2713.\\n- build_input_items \u2713.\\n- zif_pia_00_llm~chat answer: output array \u2713.\\n- Terminal/turns classes (ZCL_PIA_30_*) may parse too \u2014 can't read? I could read them but not necessary; string-aware scanning is strictly more correct. But there's one risk: entries where a string contains an unbalanced... no \u2014 proper JSON always has balanced quotes; case-A scanning is the standard correct JSON structural scan. For MALFORMED input (unclosed string), new behavior: everything after the unclosed `\\\"` is \\\"in string\\\" \u2192 braces ignored \u2192 e.g. BALANCED_UNCLOSED test in JSON_UTIL testclasses! There's an existing test BALANCED_UNCLOSED \u2014 what does it assert? Unknown source. Hmm. Let me think about what BALANCED_UNCLOSED likely tests: extract_balanced with unclosed braces \u2014 e.g. key found, open found, no close \u2192 current returns rest to end. With my change: same (loop ends at strlen-1, depth\\u003e0, rv_ = rest from start... wait current code: `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen(lv_rest) - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0` then `lv_len = lv_pos - lv_start + 1; rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len)`. If depth never 0, lv_pos ends at strlen-1 \u2192 rv_ = from lv_start to end. My version keeps that \u2713. If the unclosed test involves strings... e.g. `{\\\"a\\\":\\\"b\\\"` no close brace \u2014 scan: `{` d1, `\\\"a\\\"` in/out, `:` `\\\"b\\\"` in/out, end \u2192 depth 1 \u2192 to end \u2713 same as before.\\n\\nRisky existing tests: BALANCED_OBJECT, BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY, BALANCED_MISSING_KEY, BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET, BALANCED_UNCLOSED \u2014 my change must keep them green. \\n- BALANCED_OBJECT: e.g. extract_balanced('{\\\"a\\\":{\\\"b\\\":1}}','a','{','}') \u2192 rest from `\\\"a\\\"`: first `{` \u2192 scan: d1, `\\\"b\\\"` in/out, `}` d0 \u2192 `{\\\"b\\\":1}` \u2713 same.\\n- BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY: nested braces/arrays \u2014 with no strings or balanced strings \u2192 same result \u2713. If it has strings with brackets inside... e.g. `{\\\"a\\\":[\\\"x]y\\\"]}` \u2014 old would count `[`? open is `[`: `[\\\"x]y\\\"]` \u2014 old: `[` d1, then `]` inside string \u2192 d0 \u2192 WRONG short extraction `[\\\"x]`; new: `\\\"` in_str, `]` ignored, `\\\"` out, `]` d0 \u2192 full \u2713. If the existing test asserts the OLD (buggy) short behavior it would break \u2014 but such a test asserting buggy behavior is unlikely; more likely it tests nested arrays/objects without tricky strings. Accept risk.\\n- EXTRACT_* (extract_str) untouched \u2713.\\n- ESCAPE/UNESCAPE/TO_INT/HEXVAL untouched \u2713.\\n\\nSPLIT entries tests \u2014 are there any? The 30 methods listed from the earlier run: ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR, ESCAPE_QUOTES, ESCAPE_BACKSLASH, ESCAPE_NEWLINE, ESCAPE_NONASCII, ESCAPE_MIXED, UNESCAPE_CTRL, UNESCAPE_BACKSLASH_QUOTE, UNESCAPE_HEX_KNOWN, UNESCAPE_HEX_OTHER, ROUNDTRIP_NONASCII, EXTRACT_SIMPLE, EXTRACT_ESCAPED_QUOTE, EXTRACT_MISSING, BALANCED_OBJECT, BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY, BALANCED_MISSING_KEY, BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET, BALANCED_UNCLOSED, TO_INT_*, TO_HEX4_*, HEXVAL_*. \\n\\nNo SPLIT_ENTRIES tests visible! Good \u2014 the test class clearly doesn't cover split_entries (that's why the bug slipped through). So modifying split_entries is safe re existing tests. And BALANCED_* tests: my extract_balanced change must preserve BALANCED_OBJECT, BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY, BALANCED_MISSING_KEY, BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET, BALANCED_UNCLOSED. Missing key: no key found \u2192 RETURN empty \u2713. No open bracket: key found, no `[` \u2192 empty \u2713. Unclosed: as analyzed \u2713.\\n\\nOne more consideration on extract_balanced: FIND lv_pat IN iv_json \u2014 searches `\\\"key\\\"` \u2014 for 'arguments' as object fallback etc. Keep.\\n\\nNow \u2014 also should I make the FIND FIRST occurrence of iv_open skip strings? E.g. extract_balanced(key='output'): rest = `\\\"output\\\":[...]` fine. Edge: key at end of a string value... ignore, minimal.\\n\\nAlright \u2014 also check: does anything rely on the OLD overrunning behavior? E.g. executor mock LLM... Let me check the executor: does ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR have a mock that returns tool call JSON? The earlier read: executor uses zif_pia_00_llm injected. There's likely a test/mock class somewhere (ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_MOCK?) whose testclasses... Actually the executor's tests \u2014 unknown. My changes only make parsing more correct. If a mock builds arguments WITH braces inside strings, old code overran \u2014 tests presumably pass now, so mocks likely avoid the pattern. New code: extract_str path \u2014 for well-formed string arguments, identical result. \u2713 No behavior change except bug cases.\\n\\nOne subtle difference in the extract_str path: OLD returned the balanced substring then unescape. For arguments containing NO braces at all? e.g. `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"X\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 balanced: first `{` after key is content `{`... same result. For arguments that DON'T start with `{` (e.g. plain text, or empty): `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"\\\"` \u2192 extract_balanced finds NO `{` \u2192 empty; extract_str returns empty too (matches empty) \u2192 unescape(\\\"\\\") = \\\"\\\" \u2014 same \u2713. For `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"hello\\\"` (no braces): old \u2192 no `{` found \u2192 initial. new \u2192 \\\"hello\\\". Difference! But is that used anywhere? Tools parse arguments as JSON; \\\"hello\\\" wouldn't parse anyway. Registry invoke passes raw string. Hmm, could a replay transcript contain non-JSON arguments? The record path stores what was parsed. Empty vs \\\"hello\\\" \u2014 edge, acceptable. Actually wait: could arguments legitimately be `{}`? extract_str: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{}\\\"` \u2014 hmm, `\\\"{}\\\"` as string value: content is `{}`... wait the JSON would be `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{}\\\"` meaning arguments string = `{}`. extract_str \u2192 `{}` \u2192 fine \u2713.\\n\\nWhat about arguments containing escaped backslash before quote at end... PCRE `(?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*` handles \u2713.\\n\\nOK. Also update the doc comment for split_entries mentioning string-awareness.\\n\\nNow, do I need to fix `unescape` for anything? No.\\n\\nLet me also double check `escape` isn't involved in the bug... The junk in arguments is a PARSE issue. \u2713\\n\\nWrite the testclasses include for LLM_HTTP now. Full source:\\n\\n```abap\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    CONSTANTS:\\n      c_src TYPE string VALUE `CLASS zcl_x. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.`.\\n\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_source FOR TESTING RAISING cx_static_check.\\n    METHODS resp_two_calls_brace_in_source FOR TESTING RAISING cx_static_check.\\nENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nHmm \u2014 building the body: I want the JSON to embed the source with quotes. Let me build the arguments escaped content manually. The arguments JSON (unescaped): `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\"}`. Escaped for embedding as a JSON string value: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}`. In the test I can construct via string templates. Simplest: hardcode the full body as ABAP literal with '...' quoting, doubling internal single quotes.\\n\\nBody chat:\\n```\\n{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}\\n]}}]}\\n```\\n(without newlines \u2014 concat with \\u0026\\u0026 across lines).\\n\\nIn ABAP literal: every `\\\"` is fine, every `\\\\` fine, `'` \u2192 `''`. So: `'... lv_open = ''{''. ENDCLASS.\\\\'...'`. Wait the `\\\\\\\"` after ENDCLASS. is `\\\\` + `\\\"` \u2014 both literal chars, fine.\\n\\nExpected arguments (after unescape): `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\"}` \u2192 in ABAP literal: `'{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = ''{''. ENDCLASS.\\\"}'`.\\n\\nBody responses:\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"object\\\":\\\"response\\\",\\\"output\\\":[\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_1\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"},\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_2\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}\\n],\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2}}\\n```\\n\\nAssertions per test: 2 calls; call1 name write_source; call1 arguments exact; call2 name read_object; call2 arguments `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_Y\\\"}`.\\n\\nNote: in the responses format I put arguments AFTER status (as in real API it's usually last) to make the junk-tail symptom explicit (`\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`). Actually in the user report the junk is `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 meaning in their transcript call_id and status came AFTER arguments. My ordering: call_id before, status after \u2192 junk would be `\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`-ish. Either way. Let me match the report more closely: order: type, id, name, arguments, call_id, status? The report junk `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` implies arguments then call_id then status. Let me use that exact order in the responses test: {\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}. Then with OLD code the overrun junk is exactly `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` + `},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",...}` (merged). Nice \u2014 mirrors the report.\\n\\nNote parse_calls_responses checks `lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'` \u2014 present \u2713. And id: extract_str 'call_id' \u2713.\\n\\nAlso chat format: keep as standard (function nested).\\n\\nNow also think: should I add a test for split_entries directly? Can't (JSON_UTIL testclasses untouchable). The two LLM_HTTP tests cover it.\\n\\nAlso \u2014 do I need RAISING? assert_equals doesn't raise; lt_calls[ 1 ] with index \u2014 if fewer entries, raises cx_sy_itab_line_not_found \u2192 test errors (red) \u2014 acceptable; but to be clean use RAISING cx_static_check? cx_sy_itab_line_not_found is dynamic \u2014 no static RAISING needed. Just don't declare RAISING. Actually table expression exceptions are not static check-relevant in default (they're catchable dynamic). Keep methods without RAISING.\\n\\nNow the executor path \u2014 the user said \\\"PIA runs only the first call\\\". The root is parsing \u2713. Fix covers it.\\n\\nLet me now write the files.\\n\\nStep 1: write testclasses for LLM_HTTP + activate.\\nStep 2 (next turn): run_tests LLM_HTTP \u2192 expect RED (2 fails).\\nStep 3: write fixed JSON_UTIL main + activate; write fixed LLM_HTTP main + activate.\\nStep 4: run_tests both \u2192 LLM_HTTP green (2/2), JSON_UTIL 29 pass + ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR fail (unchanged).\\n\\nActually I can do step 1 and step 3 writes in fewer turns, but the user explicitly wants to SEE the test red first. So: this turn \u2192 write testclasses, activate. Next turn \u2192 run tests (red). Then fix. \\n\\nWait \u2014 order of ops: write_source(testclasses) then activate(L LM_HTTP). Activation publishes both includes? The class activation makes new code live. run_tests then uses published code. After activate, \\\"if activation says publish pending, finish turn and run tests next turn\\\". Let me do write+activate now.\\n\\nOne more check of my test body strings \u2014 build carefully.\\n\\nChat body (single line via \\u0026\\u0026):\\nlv_body =\\n`{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}`\\n\\nUsing backquote string literals? ABAP string templates with | | or backquotes `...` \u2014 backquote literals: everything literal, no escaping of ' needed! In ABAP, `...` is a string literal where ' doesn't need doubling and \\\" is fine. But `\\\\` is literal \u2713. So backticks are PERFECT for this: no doubling needed. Does open-abap/OSG support backquotes? Standard ABAP yes. The codebase uses backticks elsewhere (e.g. VALUE `PIA_LLM`, `\\\\\\\\`). \u2713 Use backticks.\\n\\nSo:\\n```abap\\nDATA lv_body TYPE string.\\nlv_body = `{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":null,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}}]}`.\\n```\\nBacktick string spanning multiple lines via \\u0026\\u0026 \u2014 each segment in backticks \u2713.\\n\\nExpected:\\n```abap\\nDATA lv_exp1 TYPE string.\\nlv_exp1 = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\"}`.\\n```\\n\\nResponses body:\\n```abap\\nlv_body = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"resp_1\\\",\\\"object\\\":\\\"response\\\",\\\"output\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"fc_2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}],` \\u0026\\u0026\\n          `\\\"model\\\":\\\"gpt\\\",\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1,\\\"output_tokens\\\":2}}`.\\n```\\n\\nAssertions:\\n- assert_equals exp=2 act=lines( lt_calls )\\n- assert_equals exp='write_source' act=lt_calls[ 1 ]-name\\n- assert_equals exp=lv_exp1 act=lt_calls[ 1 ]-arguments\\n- assert_equals exp='read_object' act=lt_calls[ 2 ]-name\\n- assert_equals exp='{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_Y\\\"}' act=lt_calls[ 2 ]-arguments\\n- maybe assert lt_calls[1]-id = 'c1' (chat: id; responses: call_id). chat test: id c1; resp test: id c1 via call_id.\\n\\nAlso assert the junk-tail absence: assert_char_np? Use assert_equals exact \u2014 sufficient. Maybe add explicit: cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_false( act = boolc( lt_calls[1]-arguments CS 'status' ) ) \u2014 redundant; skip. Keep tests focused.\\n\\nAlso, is `content\\\":null` in chat body an issue for ev_answer? parse_calls_chat doesn't extract content \u2713.\\n\\nTest class name: LTCL_PARSE_CALLS. Also maybe add a third test: two calls where SECOND has the brace (ensures no merge regardless of position)? Good extra coverage \u2014 cheap. Add RESP_BRACE_IN_SECOND_CALL? Keep two tests plus maybe one split-focused via chat: entry with brace in second call. I'll add it \u2014 more evidence the fix is positional-agnostic.\\n\\nActually keep it tight: 3 tests (chat first-call brace, resp first-call brace, resp second-call brace). \\n\\nNow write. Also FOR TESTING duration short risk harmless \u2713.\\n\\nAfter that, activate LLM_HTTP. Then next turn run_tests \u2192 red.\\n\\nLet me write the testclasses source now.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007194610f52e5962a1f04965\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I've located the root cause. In `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL`:\\n\\n- `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` count braces **without being aware of JSON strings**. A lone `{` inside the arguments string (e.g. from `lv_open = '{'.`) inflates the depth, so the first entry's closing `}` doesn't bring depth to 0: the first entry swallows the second one (second call lost), and the arguments extraction runs past the value's end, picking up `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` (the reported junk).\\n\\nAlso, `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP` has no test classes yet (0 methods ran earlier), so I can safely add a fresh testclasses include there \u2014 `ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR` lives in `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL`, which I won't touch at the testclasses level.\\n\\nWriting the reproduction tests first:\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007194610f52e5962a1f04965\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"\\\\\\\" Tool-call parsing regression tests.\\\\n\\\\\\\" Bug: a lone '{' inside a string literal of the first call's arguments made\\\\n\\\\\\\" split_entries merge both entries (second call lost) and made the arguments\\\\n\\\\\\\" extraction overrun into '\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":...,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}'.\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\\\n  DURATION SHORT RISK LEVEL HARMLESS.\\\\n\\\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\\\n    CONSTANTS c_src TYPE string VALUE `CLASS z. lv_open = '{'. ENDCLASS.`.\\\\n\\\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_first  FOR TESTING.\\\\n    METHODS resp_two_calls_brace_in_first  FOR TESTING.\\\\n    METHODS resp_two_calls_brace_in_second FOR TESTING.\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD chat_two_calls_brace_in_first.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_exp1 TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body = `{\\\\\\\"choices\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"content\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_source\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"read_object\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\"}}]}}]}`.\\\\n    lv_exp1 = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\"}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg = 'both tool calls must be parsed' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1'          act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-id ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_source' act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = lv_exp1       act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'arguments must end at the JSON, no trailing junk' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'read_object' act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}` act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-arguments ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD resp_two_calls_brace_in_first.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_exp1 TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body = `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resp_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"object\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"response\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"fc_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_source\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"fc_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"read_object\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}],` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"m\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"usage\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"input_tokens\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"output_tokens\\\\\\\":2}}`.\\\\n    lv_exp1 = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\"}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg = 'both tool calls must be parsed' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1'           act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-id ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_source' act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = lv_exp1        act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'arguments must end at the JSON, no trailing junk' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'read_object'  act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\"}` act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-arguments ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD resp_two_calls_brace_in_second.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_exp2 TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body = `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"resp_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"object\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"response\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"fc_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"read_object\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_Y\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"fc_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_source\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}],` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"m\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"usage\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"input_tokens\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\"output_tokens\\\\\\\":2}}`.\\\\n    lv_exp2 = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 c_src \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\"}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg = 'both tool calls must be parsed' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'read_object'  act = lt_calls[ 1 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_source' act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-name ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = lv_exp2        act = lt_calls[ 2 ]-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'arguments must end at the JSON, no trailing junk' ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228033894463831530\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228033894463831530\",\"name\":\"write_source\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":18717,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":9408},\"output_tokens\":10820,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":9297},\"total_tokens\":29537}}
